WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.520
Welcome.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:05.690
Welcome.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:10.040
It's awesome to
see everyone here.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.830
This is the third
and final event

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:16.970
sponsored by the McDermott
Award for Arts at MIT.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.610
My name's Meejin Yoon,
I'm head of the Department

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:21.290
of Architecture.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:26.010
I'd like to welcome everyone
across the auditorium

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.620
this evening.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.780
I'd like to make a
special thanks, of course,

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:35.090
to the Council for Arts at
MIT for sponsoring the award

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:39.710
and arts at MIT for organizing
all these amazing panels

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:44.450
and residency events
throughout this year.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.280
This semester, we've
been talking a lot

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.270
about the future,
multiple futures.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:53.180
The future of the library,
the future of campus,

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:56.240
and today, the
future of the museum.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.980
For MIT, these topics
are not abstract musings

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:05.570
about possible things to come
but really timely, critical,

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:10.040
and defining as we literally
reimagine and reinvent

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.400
our library, our
campus, and our museum

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.420
today for the immediate future.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.720
While the term future
is arguably overused,

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.610
I would like to use
this opportunity

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.210
to actually unpack
and open up this term.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:33.230
Beyond a speculative open ended
and positivist projection,

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:35.990
I think the term
actually allows us

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:39.920
to probe deeper into our
cultural questions and values

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:41.270
today.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.690
In the words of science fiction
novelist, William Gibson,

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.160
the future is already here.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.340
It's just unevenly distributed.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:51.710
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:56.960
In looking for this unevenly
distributed present feature,

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.360
we have assembled
an esteemed group

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.870
of museum directors,
architects, and artists

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:06.380
to give us a perspective on
the contemporary issues facing

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:11.930
the museum and what is at stake
for the future of culture.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.810
The museum is not just a
building but, of course,

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:16.640
an institution.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.510
As institutions, museums
are collections, archives,

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:24.680
think tanks, agents and they
can tell stories, provoke,

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:26.270
or canonize.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:29.930
They are both cultural
condensers and catalysts.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:34.070
We know very well the recent
museum boom, the Bilbao effect,

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:39.320
launched an intense worldwide
museum-building campaign,

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:44.180
with museums as stand-ins or
indicators of cultural maturity

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.150
or arrival.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:50.150
How can they be
not only indicators

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:53.150
of cultural maturity,
but actually catalysts

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:56.480
for both cultural and
social change, both inside

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.030
and outside the museum walls?

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:02.780
How can architecture play a role
as a mediator between artists,

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.730
institution, and public?

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.100
Between the institution
and the city?

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.550
And between the
collections and its public?

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:14.240
Amongst the speakers, there are
a number of incredible projects

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.740
completed or underway, which
speak to these questions

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:26.480
and the limits and possibilities
of futures as institutions that

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.690
intersect and transform
the way we operate

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:34.340
and curate our world.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:37.100
Our speakers need
no introduction

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:41.360
and so I have
abbreviated their bios

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.620
so that we can hear from them
directly as soon as possible.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:48.290
Our first speaker
will be David Adjaye.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.450
David Adjaye, you know well.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:53.810
He's spoken to us many times
throughout the semester.

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:57.050
He's the recipient of
the 2016 McDermott Award

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:00.050
for Arts at MIT.

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:03.530
He's an internationally
celebrated architect.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.590
His recently completed
works, including

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:10.760
libraries in Washington, DC, the
SKOLKOVO School of Management

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:15.110
in Moscow, and the Museum of
Contemporary Art in Denver.

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:17.240
Adjaye Associates
is currently working

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:21.110
on projects in Rwanda,
Ganda, India, London,

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:24.140
Lebanon, and New York City.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:28.970
Yesterday, he and Zena Howard of
Perkins and Will Freelon Group

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:32.180
discussed their soon
to open, eagerly

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:35.600
anticipated Smithsonian
National Museum

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.520
of African-American History
and Culture with our students.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:46.460
The museum will open very
soon, shortly this fall.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:49.460
He has, in his last
lecture, shared with us

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:53.180
how architects play a role in
storytelling and institution

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:55.130
building.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:57.620
Thelma Golden is the
director and chief

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:00.650
curator of the Studio
Museum in Harlem,

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:03.850
an art museum founded in 1968.

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:06.820
His mission is to serve
as the nexus of artists

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:09.860
of African descent
locally, nationally,

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:11.650
and internationally.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:16.360
Under her leadership, the museum
has gained increasing renown

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:18.640
as a center for
contemporary art,

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:23.500
innovative education, and a
site for diverse audiences.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:26.950
Golden was previously a
curator at the Whitney Museum,

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:30.830
where she organized numerous
groundbreaking exhibitions,

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:36.520
including the 1994 Black Male
Representations of Masculinity

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:39.040
in American Art.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:41.710
Thelma Golden has referred
to the museum as a think

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:45.820
tank and exhibitions
as a white paper,

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:49.870
with the idea that museums
could forecast a wiser

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:53.110
and more inclusive society.

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:57.880
Charles Renfro is partner at
Diller Scofidio + Renfro, also

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:01.360
a renowned interdisciplinary
design studio and architecture

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:02.320
firm.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:08.410
Diller Scofidio is the recipient
of the 1999 McDermott Award

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:11.110
here that MIT.

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:13.990
Charles' early projects
include the Institute

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:16.420
of Contemporary
Art here in Boston,

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:19.720
the Blur Building in
Switzerland, the Brown Creative

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:23.060
Arts Center, and the
Juilliard in New York.

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:26.240
He's currently building
major museums, libraries,

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:29.890
and institutions in
China, Russia, and Brazil.

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:33.910
DSR's current proposal for
the culture shed in New York

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:37.720
demonstrates that architects
can be proactive instigators,

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:41.350
establishing program
and institution

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:46.360
building by rethinking
relationships of organization

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:47.605
and curatorial freedom.

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:54.130
Jill Medvedow is the Ellen
Matilda Pos' director

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:59.350
of the Institute of Contemporary
Art here in Boston since 1998.

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:03.220
As director, she built the
first new art museum in Boston

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:05.290
in nearly a century.

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:08.980
Under her direction, the
ICA expanded its exhibitions

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:10.750
and performance program.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:14.590
It became a collections,
a collecting institution

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:18.490
and has become a national
leader in arts education.

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:21.580
The ICA's recent exhibit,
Leap Before You Look--

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:25.240
Black Mountain
College 1933 to '57,

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:27.790
exemplifies the
ICA's integration

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:32.620
of exhibition, performance,
and community engagement.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:37.840
Lorna Simpson is a
well-known conceptual artist

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:40.160
from Brooklyn, New York.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:42.430
She's known for her
large scale photography

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:45.400
and text-based works,
videos, and drawings

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:49.000
that confront and challenge the
conventional views of gender

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.540
identity, culture,
history, and memory.

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:56.230
Her work has been exhibited
at the Museum of Modern Art,

00:07:56.230 --> 00:07:58.750
the Museum of Contemporary
Art in Chicago,

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:01.720
the Walker Art Center
in Minneapolis, as well

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:07.030
as Documenta11, and the
56th Venice Biennale.

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:10.690
Last, I'm going to
introduce Timothy Hyde, who

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:13.420
is an associate professor
of architecture history

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:15.230
here at MIT.

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:17.530
His research focuses
on the intersection

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:20.050
of architecture and politics.

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:22.570
He is the author of
Constitutional Modernism--

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:25.510
Architecture and
Civil Society in Cuba

00:08:25.510 --> 00:08:29.830
and is the chair of the
Aggregate Architectural History

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:31.510
Collaborative.

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:34.059
That is our esteemed
panel and I'd

00:08:34.059 --> 00:08:39.350
like to first welcome David up
to share with us his thoughts.

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:46.210
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:48.760
Hi, everyone.

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:50.740
It's great to be
back in this room,

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:52.472
having these conversations.

00:08:52.472 --> 00:08:54.430
I'm not going to make
any grand statements yet,

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:56.597
I think that we're going
to leave that till the end.

00:08:56.597 --> 00:09:00.640
But I want to take you
through a series of projects

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.065
to ask a series of
questions and to maybe show

00:09:04.065 --> 00:09:05.440
a series of
trajectories that are

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:10.180
happening at the beginning of
this millennia, this century.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:12.520
I'm going to go
from small to things

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.280
that are also on
the board to things

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:19.060
that are just completing.

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:21.697
I want to start with
this project, which

00:09:21.697 --> 00:09:22.780
is called Rivington Place.

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:26.007
It's a project that
was completed in 2007.

00:09:26.007 --> 00:09:28.090
It's an important project
for me because in a way,

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:31.930
it was about the--

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:35.950
what I would call the guerilla
work of a profound educator,

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:40.330
intellectual teacher, author,
Stuart Hall, who took it

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:47.320
upon himself to really talk
about the lack of scholarship

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:51.270
and archive around the
Black contribution to Europe

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:53.440
and the influence
of the Caribbean,

00:09:53.440 --> 00:10:00.100
et cetera, in the arts discourse
in Europe, specifically.

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:03.190
It really was a kind
of a guerilla operation

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:08.740
for many decades, before the
Arts Council, a body in the UK

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:12.040
finally recognized that this was
an important institution that

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:16.030
had created a new
body of work that

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:19.073
needed its own institution.

00:10:19.073 --> 00:10:20.740
They were given a
grant and a commission

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:23.020
to finally set up a
base for the archive

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:30.220
but also to create a dissolved
hybrid institution, which

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:35.410
is between the archive,
the library, the community

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:38.080
space, the education
space, the workspace.

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:43.060
The site that were
given was placed

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:47.470
in this very historic
part of London and Hoxton,

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:50.170
but in the 17th century
and 18th century Hoxton.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:57.340
This idea of a new body
within this context

00:10:57.340 --> 00:11:03.040
was a fascinating proposition.

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:05.530
Of course, I'm not talking
about it as a lecture

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:08.200
but just to give you a clue,
I think this section gives you

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:11.110
the clue of the diversity of the
types that I'm talking about.

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:14.320
Everything from
acknowledging also

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:16.540
the industrial heritage
of the area, which

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:18.940
is next to the
Georgian heritage,

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:22.600
and the different
programmatic functions that

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:25.827
are in the building
in terms of education,

00:11:25.827 --> 00:11:27.160
et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:30.670
This building is a hybrid
that's working within this very

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:39.010
particular historical
context and uses

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:40.150
computational technology.

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:43.685
It uses the idea of being
able to create a system where

00:11:43.685 --> 00:11:46.060
there's a relationship that's
been made with this context

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:47.935
but also there's another
discourse happening.

00:11:47.935 --> 00:11:50.230
What you're looking at is
an image which is actually

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:52.870
about a foreshortened view.

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:54.260
The grid is not equal.

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:59.920
It expands from 2
feet to 15 feet.

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:03.763
The idea was to look at this
idea of optics and perception

00:12:03.763 --> 00:12:04.930
as a way to make a building.

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:08.440
This is the building looking
as though it is a set up.

00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:12.850
It's this idea of an institution
that's in this context

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:17.470
but is questioning and
asking about the context

00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:22.570
and talking about the
nature of the condition.

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:24.920
In its very general sense,
it's a very large window,

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:27.640
which you see at the base, and
that really becomes a window

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:29.200
directly onto the streets.

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:32.170
A shopfront of
exhibitions and galleries,

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:39.880
which is then a space for
white or black space projects.

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:43.090
Education and screening rooms,
small, very small screening

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:45.700
rooms and exhibition spaces.

00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:48.520
The archive, which
is in the heart

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:52.700
of the plan, which has
the biggest archive

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:59.950
and has become the place
where documentation

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:02.890
about the activities of
the diaspora in Europe

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:04.690
and the Caribbean is stored.

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:07.540
And has become a very important
academic resource in London.

00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:12.910
Then there's obviously
the office areas

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:15.400
and the way in which the
building distributes itself.

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:20.140
The studios that are up above
for like-minded institutions,

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:25.270
which are part of the mix
of the life of the building.

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:27.430
I'll move to something
more familiar.

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:30.250
This is a project
in Denver, which

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:34.450
is to make a type
of ICA institution.

00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:35.853
Those of you who--

00:13:35.853 --> 00:13:36.520
Is this working?

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:37.240
Is this gone?

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:37.930
OK, fine.

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:39.394
[CHUCKLES]

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:47.470
The ICA is a museum of
contemporary art in Denver.

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:51.413
The thinking with the
board and the director

00:13:51.413 --> 00:13:53.080
at the time, Sydney
Payton, was to think

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:55.990
about the notion of the
contemporary art space

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:59.180
not as a flexible
large container

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:00.490
but the exact opposite.

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:03.280
To return back to the
practice of artists

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:06.910
and to look at the scales
of practice and production

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:11.890
and to see if that could
be somehow constructionally

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:15.730
narrated into a scenario
where instead of flexibility,

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:18.580
you had a fixed set of
proportions and geometries,

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:21.670
which would allow a
diversity of art practice

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:23.440
to flourish within
it without worrying

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:25.510
about the reconfiguring.

00:14:25.510 --> 00:14:30.040
The research was all about
how to look at different art

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:32.680
practices and different
spaces that artists work

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:35.380
in from their apartments
through to factories,

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:39.970
through to institutional
spaces, and to configure

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:43.240
a series of volumes that are
stacked, almost in a very

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:46.720
classical way with a
cellar, a middle, and a top,

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:51.610
to create a house that in its
nature, is a house which is,

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:55.360
in a way, distributed almost
like a little mini art

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:58.390
collage, art city collage.

00:14:58.390 --> 00:15:01.970
The building is basically
in a downtown area,

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:05.260
which was a very
brick-dominated architecture.

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:07.000
The idea was to--

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:08.098
It does two things.

00:15:08.098 --> 00:15:09.640
It was the first
[INAUDIBLE] building

00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:12.790
in this country for the arts.

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:16.570
But it was also about making
a diffuse building, which

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:18.880
created a neutral plane.

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:23.140
The reason was that, in a way,
the downtown area was emerging

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:27.250
as a new camp, a new area
that was to be rebuilt.

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:29.230
The building wanted
to create a restraint

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:34.018
frame against the new commercial
and residential high density,

00:15:34.018 --> 00:15:34.810
which is happening.

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:35.727
And now it's happened.

00:15:35.727 --> 00:15:38.500
If you're in Denver,
you can see it.

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:42.510
When it was made, it was
a very isolated element.

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:45.820
Now, with the context of the
hyper commercial architecture,

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:51.400
it becomes a restraint and
entropy within this condition.

00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:53.890
The building is expressed,
the geometry and the volumes

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:56.440
are expressed, and the
qualities of light in Denver--

00:15:56.440 --> 00:15:58.870
That image at the front,
Denver's about a mile high.

00:15:58.870 --> 00:15:59.860
--is also enjoyed.

00:15:59.860 --> 00:16:03.070
The geography and the
atmosphere and the environment

00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:04.150
of that place is enjoyed.

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:06.160
Then you have different
scales of spaces

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:08.800
where spaces where windows
bring in indirect light

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:10.390
with different proportions.

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:14.350
Where skylights reflect light
down into halo chambers.

00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:17.290
Where you have relationships
to the city that

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:21.160
are curated to create diaphanous
spaces which are diffuse,

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:23.020
where the chambers in
these sort of spaces

00:16:23.020 --> 00:16:26.290
create almost a medieval
compressed city for events

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:28.060
looking through
the entire section

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:29.338
and program of the building.

00:16:29.338 --> 00:16:30.880
Where you have
education spaces which

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:33.790
differentiate their
quality and create

00:16:33.790 --> 00:16:36.490
destinations for the community.

00:16:36.490 --> 00:16:38.740
Education spaces that
are journeys at the top

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:41.380
of the building, which
also talk about the city.

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:42.880
Extraordinary
gallery spaces that

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:45.280
are 30 feet high that talk
about the scale of sculpture

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:47.470
and the condition
of contemporary art

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:50.620
in terms of scales,
large and small forms.

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:52.720
Then respite, the
notion of just a place

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:58.930
to be in the city to enjoy
one's location and one's place

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:00.025
and to reflect.

00:17:05.138 --> 00:17:06.680
This is a project
I'm working on now.

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:10.339
We're not allowed to
show too many pictures.

00:17:10.339 --> 00:17:12.630
This is the Studio Museum,
it's a work in progress.

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:14.338
But the Studio Museum,
if you don't know,

00:17:14.338 --> 00:17:16.010
it is a very
important institution

00:17:16.010 --> 00:17:21.089
that obviously was just
described just a second ago.

00:17:21.089 --> 00:17:24.589
But the idea that this
institution, which

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:29.770
in its essence essentially
is the radicality and Thelma

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:31.520
Golden has talked about
it being the think

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:32.720
tank, but the radicalities.

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:34.428
This is one of the
few institutions which

00:17:34.428 --> 00:17:44.060
permeates the artist as a very
constant, live scenario that

00:17:44.060 --> 00:17:47.810
is continually questioning and
in dialogue with institution.

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:49.700
The Studio Museum
literally is a space

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:53.600
of studios for artists to
be present in the building.

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:55.700
And to interact with the
curators and the public

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:58.160
and to make works which
are celebrated yearly.

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:01.940
The building is also a testimony
about a certain engagement

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:04.040
with the context
of Harlem and how

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:08.570
that engagement with that
public is to be programmed

00:18:08.570 --> 00:18:11.030
and in that porosity,
to be enjoyed.

00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:13.250
What you're looking at is,
really, a sectional study.

00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:14.750
I like using these
sectional studies

00:18:14.750 --> 00:18:17.120
to talk about the notions,
the scales of the civic,

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:20.210
the public, the art spaces,
the compressed education

00:18:20.210 --> 00:18:22.200
spaces, and the
archive and offices,

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:24.770
et cetera, that are held
within the building.

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:27.620
This notion of a building which
dissolves at its ground plain

00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:29.690
but also expresses the
studio and education

00:18:29.690 --> 00:18:31.815
elements as
programmatic typologies,

00:18:31.815 --> 00:18:33.440
which are stacked on
top of each other,

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:36.560
is really the agenda behind it.

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:39.290
Also, we're hoping that as
the building sets itself up,

00:18:39.290 --> 00:18:41.540
it creates an entropy because
Harlem has been re-zoned

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:44.510
and it's going through
a radical rebuilding.

00:18:44.510 --> 00:18:46.550
That the building
also can maybe become

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:52.130
a figure that sets a certain
cultural permanence and entropy

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:56.740
within the flux context of 125th
Street, which the location is.

00:18:56.740 --> 00:18:59.630
Then lastly, the
Smithsonian, which

00:18:59.630 --> 00:19:01.910
I've shown before but
I just will quickly

00:19:01.910 --> 00:19:05.930
go through with you, which
is on the Washington Mall.

00:19:05.930 --> 00:19:13.580
Really is a 200-year struggle
to place a narrative museum

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:17.360
on the heart of the
nation's monumental core.

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:21.655
One which would celebrate the
narrative of the contribution

00:19:21.655 --> 00:19:23.030
of the African-American
community

00:19:23.030 --> 00:19:24.680
to the identity of America.

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:27.885
Really, the building is this
lens to understand that.

00:19:27.885 --> 00:19:30.260
This idea that finally, at
the beginning of 21st century,

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:33.763
it's very clear that this
notion of the narrative

00:19:33.763 --> 00:19:35.180
and understanding
the cosmopolitan

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:37.408
condition of our urban
cities as we identify

00:19:37.408 --> 00:19:39.950
and the complexity of the matrix
of people that come together

00:19:39.950 --> 00:19:43.970
to create the Metropolitan,
requires a kind of unfolding

00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:46.410
of the story, of all
the constitutions, all

00:19:46.410 --> 00:19:48.890
the constituents of the
parts to create a richer

00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:51.440
dialogue and more tolerance.

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:55.010
I think in a way,
a healthy relation

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:58.070
in terms of understanding
origins and sources.

00:19:58.070 --> 00:20:01.370
The building is a museum, yes,
but it's a narrative museum.

00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:03.470
It's not a fragment of a story.

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:05.990
It's not about a trauma
but it is about an entire

00:20:05.990 --> 00:20:11.480
picking a moment for that
journey to have begun to now.

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:14.560
It really is a building
which is in three parts.

00:20:14.560 --> 00:20:17.630
It talks about history, then
it talks about the present,

00:20:17.630 --> 00:20:19.910
and it talks about
looking to the future.

00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:24.440
This tripartite nature is imbued
in the nature of the building.

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:27.470
The project attempts to
create not just a container

00:20:27.470 --> 00:20:30.620
for the work but the building
whose very silhouette

00:20:30.620 --> 00:20:33.020
and materiality derives
from the context

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:35.690
of the narrative of the story.

00:20:35.690 --> 00:20:38.390
But also of the
condition of Washington

00:20:38.390 --> 00:20:41.120
in that monumental
position, the core.

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:43.550
This is the building here.

00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:47.240
Then this is the building
as it's finishing.

00:20:47.240 --> 00:20:51.020
And how it's going to work.

00:20:55.278 --> 00:20:56.214
Ta-da.

00:20:56.214 --> 00:20:57.590
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:57.590 --> 00:20:58.090
Done.

00:20:58.090 --> 00:20:58.632
There you go.

00:20:58.632 --> 00:21:00.002
[LAUGHS]

00:21:00.002 --> 00:21:02.906
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:02.906 --> 00:21:03.880
That was great.

00:21:03.880 --> 00:21:05.780
Thank you.

00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:06.455
OK, good.

00:21:06.455 --> 00:21:07.080
That was great.

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:09.100
Thelma?

00:21:09.100 --> 00:21:10.383
No?

00:21:10.383 --> 00:21:10.883
Charles?

00:21:17.140 --> 00:21:20.480
Don't start the timer
until I tell you to.

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:23.420
I'm going to get
through 20 slides first.

00:21:23.420 --> 00:21:25.190
I have 105 slides so--

00:21:25.190 --> 00:21:26.472
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:26.472 --> 00:21:27.680
I'm going to do this quickly.

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:29.150
I'm going to start my timer now.

00:21:32.120 --> 00:21:32.955
What makes a museum?

00:21:32.955 --> 00:21:34.580
I thought we'd start
with the questions

00:21:34.580 --> 00:21:37.940
because I've got 120 slides and
if I don't get to the pictures,

00:21:37.940 --> 00:21:41.540
at least you can have some
things to think about.

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:46.790
Objects, performance, events,
restaurants, education.

00:21:46.790 --> 00:21:48.260
What makes a museum?

00:21:48.260 --> 00:21:49.670
Just think about it.

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:52.230
What does the museum do?

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:53.120
Oh, wait.

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:55.060
There's a slide missing.

00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:56.320
Uh-oh.

00:21:56.320 --> 00:21:57.440
OK, slide was missing.

00:21:57.440 --> 00:22:07.060
It was supposed to say educate
enlighten and entertain.

00:22:07.060 --> 00:22:08.860
That's what that
was supposed to do.

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:09.745
What makes a museum?

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:15.540
Oh, dear.

00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:18.442
My answers are not in here.

00:22:18.442 --> 00:22:19.275
What makes a museum?

00:22:19.275 --> 00:22:21.930
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:21.930 --> 00:22:24.420
How does-- OK, how
does a museum survive?

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:25.290
This is--

00:22:25.290 --> 00:22:26.940
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:29.940
Can I put pause on because
the slides got screwed up.

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:32.130
I didn't even check beforehand.

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:35.100
Boards, the public,
foundations, government.

00:22:35.100 --> 00:22:36.960
I mean, look at those
constituent groups.

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:39.090
These are all stakeholders.

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:42.840
However do we make a
museum deal with that?

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:43.992
Who decides content?

00:22:43.992 --> 00:22:44.700
Oh, this is good.

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:46.440
They're just straightening up.

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:50.220
Directors, curators,
board members, public.

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:53.220
Everybody who puts money
into a museum has a voice.

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:55.920
How do we listen to and
wrangle those voices together

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:58.810
to make something meaningful?

00:22:58.810 --> 00:23:00.930
Who is a museum for?

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:02.625
General public, scholars.

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:08.730
Are museums really
about archiving?

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:10.200
Are they about collections?

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:12.000
Are they about civicness?

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:13.920
These are all questions
that we started

00:23:13.920 --> 00:23:18.900
pondering when we were hired
to do the ICA here in Boston.

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:23.310
The ICA had been a roving
institution, housing buildings

00:23:23.310 --> 00:23:26.880
that were not made as a museum.

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:30.780
When we were hired
in 2001 to make--

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:32.370
I think, 2001.

00:23:32.370 --> 00:23:34.920
We thought, well, what is
a museum building mean?

00:23:34.920 --> 00:23:36.390
What's the dichotomy?

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:37.650
What's the duality?

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:40.170
Here you have the
Bilbao effect that I'm

00:23:40.170 --> 00:23:42.180
sure we're going to talk
about a lot tonight.

00:23:42.180 --> 00:23:45.060
Or do you need something that's
neutral box, like the Goetz

00:23:45.060 --> 00:23:46.800
collection by Herzog de Meuron?

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:48.400
We decidedly, along
with our client,

00:23:48.400 --> 00:23:50.880
who we worked very, very
closely with, said no.

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:54.720
We're not in that discourse.

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:56.850
We're squarely outside.

00:23:56.850 --> 00:23:58.620
But one thing we
felt certain about

00:23:58.620 --> 00:24:02.010
is that the site, that beautiful
site on the waterfront,

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:04.050
was nothing but a hindrance.

00:24:04.050 --> 00:24:05.970
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:05.970 --> 00:24:07.480
For many, many ways.

00:24:07.480 --> 00:24:08.380
Many, many reasons.

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:10.600
For one thing, you
couldn't get to it.

00:24:10.600 --> 00:24:12.090
It's in a sea of parking lots.

00:24:12.090 --> 00:24:14.310
For another thing,
it's gorgeous.

00:24:14.310 --> 00:24:16.200
So how does a
museum, which is all

00:24:16.200 --> 00:24:19.530
about contemplation, about
looking, about engagement--

00:24:19.530 --> 00:24:24.720
how does a museum
take the distractions

00:24:24.720 --> 00:24:27.210
and turn them into
something positive?

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:31.558
Our museum is a
machine built to look.

00:24:31.558 --> 00:24:33.600
It does so by taking the
site and abstracting it.

00:24:33.600 --> 00:24:35.250
I'm sure many of
you have been there.

00:24:35.250 --> 00:24:36.510
It does a lot of these things.

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:39.780
It blurs it, it pixelates
it, it edits it down.

00:24:39.780 --> 00:24:42.930
Though not as much as we
had originally planned.

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:46.720
Anyway, museums are about
contemplation, often.

00:24:46.720 --> 00:24:48.720
The other thing that we
feel very strongly about

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:51.510
is that museums
are civic in nature

00:24:51.510 --> 00:24:54.690
and that's something that
they've become more recently.

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:58.410
With the ICA, we were determined
to grow the museum out

00:24:58.410 --> 00:24:59.610
of the seeds of the site.

00:24:59.610 --> 00:25:01.800
The seeds of the site
are a harbor walk

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:05.250
that was mandated by the
city of Boston, made of wood.

00:25:05.250 --> 00:25:08.190
That harbor walk was first
brought into the building

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:11.850
and then brought up
into the building

00:25:11.850 --> 00:25:13.830
in the form of a theater.

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:16.380
And a ceiling over
a public space,

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:21.030
which formed a civic base
to a more contemplative top,

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:22.320
housing the art gallery.

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:24.780
Built on a single floor
and reaching all the way

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:30.930
to the water, which required
that we change Boston's newly

00:25:30.930 --> 00:25:34.080
instituted zoning plan which
said that you could not

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:39.207
be within 25 feet of the water.

00:25:39.207 --> 00:25:40.290
Here's what it looks like.

00:25:40.290 --> 00:25:42.300
I hope you've been down there.

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:45.120
The reason that we were able
to convince the city of Boston

00:25:45.120 --> 00:25:47.880
to let us build onto
the water's edge

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:50.250
is because we gave
back a donation.

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:52.980
A donation to the city
of Boston in the form

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:55.020
of an outdoor
room, which has now

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:59.160
become one of Boston's most
celebrated outdoor spaces.

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:02.850
A space of extended programming
that the ICA has taken command

00:26:02.850 --> 00:26:06.330
of, particularly
in the warm months.

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:10.170
It's become a much
loved and very important

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:12.540
civic space in Boston.

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:15.090
Also a demonstration
project about how

00:26:15.090 --> 00:26:17.970
to take the resources
of your community

00:26:17.970 --> 00:26:23.460
in and turn them into something
more than what they simply are.

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:27.960
We're very proud of the way that
this building set an example

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:33.780
for other projects in Boston to
actively engage the waterfront.

00:26:33.780 --> 00:26:35.640
It's a very, very
simple building,

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:38.590
built on a very simple diagram
of that very harbor wall

00:26:38.590 --> 00:26:42.210
coming around and forming
a loop over a theater.

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:44.100
This is actually the
extension of the harbor

00:26:44.100 --> 00:26:46.590
walk, the theater.

00:26:46.590 --> 00:26:48.600
It was built with
two glass walls.

00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:51.990
Those walls are about
bridging between the inside

00:26:51.990 --> 00:26:54.420
and the outside, of course,
but also about taking the site

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:57.630
and having the site become a
protagonist in the presentation

00:26:57.630 --> 00:27:01.740
of the works, whether they're
visual art or performing art.

00:27:01.740 --> 00:27:04.890
Many of the performers
in the theater

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:06.810
have left the blinds
open, though you

00:27:06.810 --> 00:27:09.150
can, in fact, dark it down.

00:27:09.150 --> 00:27:10.800
Audiences have
gathered on the harbor

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:14.460
walk for free performance.

00:27:14.460 --> 00:27:17.760
The building was done
very inexpensively.

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:19.680
Not cheaply but inexpensively.

00:27:22.770 --> 00:27:25.860
The theater of
attending the museum

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:27.900
was considered in many ways.

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:30.240
One way was, how do
you get people up

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:32.415
to the top of a building
when all the galleries are

00:27:32.415 --> 00:27:33.563
on the top floor?

00:27:33.563 --> 00:27:35.480
Well, you do it by making
a piece of spectacle

00:27:35.480 --> 00:27:38.180
that, in a way, is
an artwork in itself.

00:27:38.180 --> 00:27:43.680
It's an elevator that's
glass and can hold 70 people.

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:45.580
It's a kind of
scanner in itself.

00:27:45.580 --> 00:27:48.480
It also controls the
views to and from.

00:27:48.480 --> 00:27:52.020
Actually from the museum out
through back of house spaces,

00:27:52.020 --> 00:27:55.710
through a coat check room,
through the mediatheque,

00:27:55.710 --> 00:27:59.820
to the water, to the
distracting view.

00:27:59.820 --> 00:28:03.630
It provides a space that has
been since co-opted by the ICA,

00:28:03.630 --> 00:28:09.020
ever interested in finding new
and innovative ways of engaging

00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:12.800
the public and showing
art, using the atrium

00:28:12.800 --> 00:28:14.960
in front of the elevator.

00:28:14.960 --> 00:28:18.170
The galleries themselves,
which sit on the top,

00:28:18.170 --> 00:28:20.160
are able to be naturally lit.

00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:22.460
They're also able
to be darkened down.

00:28:22.460 --> 00:28:26.810
But most importantly,
they're neutral.

00:28:26.810 --> 00:28:30.140
I showed two shows
without walls but the room

00:28:30.140 --> 00:28:33.140
was intended to be completely
flexible and divisible.

00:28:33.140 --> 00:28:33.920
It is right now.

00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:35.900
If you go, you'll
see walls throughout.

00:28:39.030 --> 00:28:44.262
The hope was that the museum is
constantly reinventing itself.

00:28:44.262 --> 00:28:45.470
I think that that's the case.

00:28:45.470 --> 00:28:49.010
The programming that
they have put on since we

00:28:49.010 --> 00:28:51.230
opened 10,000 years ago--

00:28:51.230 --> 00:28:53.240
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:53.240 --> 00:28:58.910
--10 years ago has
been spectacular.

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:01.040
Particularly, shows
like Black Mountain,

00:29:01.040 --> 00:29:04.820
which simultaneously are
entertaining and educational

00:29:04.820 --> 00:29:08.330
and scholarly.

00:29:08.330 --> 00:29:12.787
This building is it's,
we think at least,

00:29:12.787 --> 00:29:14.870
and you can be the judge
of it and you can quiz us

00:29:14.870 --> 00:29:16.970
when we sit down
later, we believe

00:29:16.970 --> 00:29:21.440
that it tries to tackle
all of the requirements--

00:29:21.440 --> 00:29:26.000
Spectacle, education,
entertainment, all of that.

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:27.890
It does so by
leveraging its site

00:29:27.890 --> 00:29:29.780
and bringing its
site in, essentially

00:29:29.780 --> 00:29:32.912
as a part of the collection.

00:29:32.912 --> 00:29:34.370
The ICA is now a
collecting museum.

00:29:34.370 --> 00:29:35.720
Well, guess what they collected?

00:29:35.720 --> 00:29:36.890
They collected their site.

00:29:39.410 --> 00:29:41.300
Among other things, by the way.

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:44.930
In Los Angeles, kind
of derelict downtown,

00:29:44.930 --> 00:29:46.640
Grand Street
redevelopment, which

00:29:46.640 --> 00:29:51.620
had started to collect a
lot of cultural jewels,

00:29:51.620 --> 00:29:54.890
including the
Music Center, built

00:29:54.890 --> 00:29:59.120
in the '60s, about the same
time as Lincoln Center.

00:29:59.120 --> 00:30:03.020
And more recently, Frank
Gehry's concert hall.

00:30:03.020 --> 00:30:07.730
But it still is a
abandoned place.

00:30:07.730 --> 00:30:09.500
It was abandoned forcibly.

00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:13.130
In fact, the architecture was
moved, literally forced out

00:30:13.130 --> 00:30:16.520
on rollers from Grand
Avenue and the Avenue

00:30:16.520 --> 00:30:19.820
was completely reconstructed
as an elevated roadway.

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:21.410
Divorced from the
ground and basically

00:30:21.410 --> 00:30:23.780
divorced from the city.

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:27.200
Los Angeles and Los
Angeles' leaders

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:30.980
have been trying to
reconnect Grand Avenue back

00:30:30.980 --> 00:30:33.590
to the city for
the three decades,

00:30:33.590 --> 00:30:37.940
four decades since they forced
it to be this elevated roadway.

00:30:37.940 --> 00:30:41.400
Essentially a freeway
cutting through the middle.

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:44.690
They have leveraged
architecture to bring life back

00:30:44.690 --> 00:30:46.260
to downtown and
to a large degree,

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:47.510
they've been quite successful.

00:30:47.510 --> 00:30:48.927
If you've been to
Los Angeles, you

00:30:48.927 --> 00:30:50.960
know how much downtown
has been changing.

00:30:50.960 --> 00:30:53.570
I even walked a few
blocks there recently

00:30:53.570 --> 00:30:56.360
and didn't feel endangered.

00:30:56.360 --> 00:30:59.300
In fact, I loved it.

00:30:59.300 --> 00:31:01.790
But all of this all
of this architecture

00:31:01.790 --> 00:31:04.010
is wonderful but it's
also challenging.

00:31:04.010 --> 00:31:07.100
It was a challenge when we
were awarded the contract

00:31:07.100 --> 00:31:11.330
to do Eli Broad's new private
museum next door to the Disney

00:31:11.330 --> 00:31:13.940
Concert Hall in a
very constrained site

00:31:13.940 --> 00:31:18.860
with a 70-foot height limit
and a 200-foot street exposure.

00:31:18.860 --> 00:31:22.460
We used the opposite approach.

00:31:22.460 --> 00:31:26.510
Rather than being shiny
and smooth and sculptural,

00:31:26.510 --> 00:31:32.060
we did something flat and
matte and light absorbing.

00:31:32.060 --> 00:31:35.630
This is a building that
has a lot of storage in it.

00:31:35.630 --> 00:31:38.450
Another issue surrounding
contemporary museums

00:31:38.450 --> 00:31:41.090
that have collections,
how do you

00:31:41.090 --> 00:31:44.420
account for the ever
increasing amount of things

00:31:44.420 --> 00:31:46.760
that you need to have?

00:31:46.760 --> 00:31:48.890
We were interested in how
to take those things that

00:31:48.890 --> 00:31:52.790
are stored in those dark
spaces and actually leverage

00:31:52.790 --> 00:31:56.720
them and turn them into part
of the museum experience.

00:31:56.720 --> 00:31:59.750
Our idea was to
enshroud those elements

00:31:59.750 --> 00:32:02.270
in a thing we call the vault.
It's sculpted, it's dark,

00:32:02.270 --> 00:32:04.460
it's almost Darth Vader-y.

00:32:04.460 --> 00:32:07.130
And then pierce it.

00:32:07.130 --> 00:32:09.560
There's a lot of
piercing in our projects.

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:12.980
Pierce it with an
escalator right

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:17.300
through the vault that delivers
you to a gallery floor that's

00:32:17.300 --> 00:32:19.610
taking the light, the
beautiful Los Angeles light,

00:32:19.610 --> 00:32:21.920
through a veil.

00:32:21.920 --> 00:32:23.510
That veil is shown here.

00:32:23.510 --> 00:32:26.600
The veil is made of
precast concrete.

00:32:26.600 --> 00:32:29.180
Each cell of which
faces due North,

00:32:29.180 --> 00:32:34.820
and so there's no direct
light into the gallery.

00:32:34.820 --> 00:32:38.000
We're very interested
in the cinematic aspect

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:42.110
of getting to the museum,
driving into the museum.

00:32:42.110 --> 00:32:48.050
We originally had a
VIP valet drop off

00:32:48.050 --> 00:32:50.057
in the lobby of the building.

00:32:50.057 --> 00:32:51.890
You drove through the
front door, same front

00:32:51.890 --> 00:32:53.390
door that the pedestrians--

00:32:53.390 --> 00:32:55.130
Ha-ha --would go through.

00:32:55.130 --> 00:32:57.740
The same people that
ticket the pedestrians

00:32:57.740 --> 00:32:59.180
drove your car
down to the garage.

00:32:59.180 --> 00:33:00.930
We thought that would
be really fantastic.

00:33:00.930 --> 00:33:03.950
Eli didn't like that so
much so that was eliminated.

00:33:03.950 --> 00:33:06.490
Nonetheless we were
interested in the theater

00:33:06.490 --> 00:33:10.240
of getting to the art.

00:33:10.240 --> 00:33:11.200
Here is the screen.

00:33:11.200 --> 00:33:14.740
We were also interested in
having the screen give glimpses

00:33:14.740 --> 00:33:17.770
of the gallery space
and even sometimes art

00:33:17.770 --> 00:33:22.750
to Los Angelenos driving
by on Grand Avenue.

00:33:22.750 --> 00:33:23.710
Here it is.

00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:26.200
In a way, it's a
very mute building

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:27.850
that has been called
the cheese grater

00:33:27.850 --> 00:33:29.878
and various other things.

00:33:29.878 --> 00:33:31.420
But it's also a very
public building.

00:33:31.420 --> 00:33:32.950
It's a free museum.

00:33:32.950 --> 00:33:34.360
I think this is
one of the issues

00:33:34.360 --> 00:33:36.220
that we need to talk about.

00:33:36.220 --> 00:33:40.390
How should we charge to
get people into museums?

00:33:40.390 --> 00:33:42.910
I'll tell you a couple of
statistics in the two minutes

00:33:42.910 --> 00:33:46.570
and 47 seconds I have left.

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:48.910
You're under the
vault, you enter,

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:53.908
you go up a 100-foot
long escalator one way.

00:33:53.908 --> 00:33:54.700
Only one escalator.

00:33:54.700 --> 00:33:56.350
You can't even see how
you're going to get out.

00:33:56.350 --> 00:33:57.730
It's probably fairly scary.

00:33:57.730 --> 00:34:01.660
And you approach art heaven,
essentially in the clouds.

00:34:01.660 --> 00:34:02.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:02.860 --> 00:34:05.650
We made, very much like
the ICA, and riffing

00:34:05.650 --> 00:34:07.570
on some of the work
we did here in Boston,

00:34:07.570 --> 00:34:10.960
a top lit room
with a partitioning

00:34:10.960 --> 00:34:12.909
system that can be brought in.

00:34:12.909 --> 00:34:18.699
This 30,000 square foot
space can be broken down

00:34:18.699 --> 00:34:19.639
in many, many ways.

00:34:19.639 --> 00:34:23.230
This is a part of the first
show that you've seen.

00:34:23.230 --> 00:34:27.130
Or you may not, but hopefully
you will get out there.

00:34:27.130 --> 00:34:29.980
You can see that
parts of the gallery

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:33.610
are actually glassed in.

00:34:33.610 --> 00:34:36.650
You can see that glass
in the very front.

00:34:36.650 --> 00:34:39.550
At that moment, you can also
go to the edge of the gallery

00:34:39.550 --> 00:34:41.560
and see these fleeting
glimpses of the city pass

00:34:41.560 --> 00:34:44.380
by on Grand Avenue below.

00:34:44.380 --> 00:34:46.219
When you finally decide
you want to leave,

00:34:46.219 --> 00:34:47.469
you can find a stair down.

00:34:47.469 --> 00:34:52.179
You can also take this
elevator, glass elevator.

00:34:52.179 --> 00:34:57.430
Both of these elements pierce
the vault. That's the elevator

00:34:57.430 --> 00:34:59.350
and then here's the stair.

00:34:59.350 --> 00:35:02.372
As you wind down a
circuitous stair,

00:35:02.372 --> 00:35:04.330
there are several moments
where you can glimpse

00:35:04.330 --> 00:35:07.030
into the storage facility.

00:35:07.030 --> 00:35:10.000
Here's the reciprocal
view, exchanging

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:11.500
your front of
house spectatorship

00:35:11.500 --> 00:35:16.540
to a back of house connoisseur.

00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:19.240
You see what's going on.

00:35:19.240 --> 00:35:21.370
In fact, there are
thousands and thousands

00:35:21.370 --> 00:35:23.800
of works that are there.

00:35:23.800 --> 00:35:26.890
They pull the racks
out to show them.

00:35:26.890 --> 00:35:28.510
Different works at
different times.

00:35:28.510 --> 00:35:31.930
We also worked on the park
next door to the Broad.

00:35:31.930 --> 00:35:36.070
In fact, took the
whole side over

00:35:36.070 --> 00:35:40.153
and have been trying to
slowly rebuild Grand Avenue.

00:35:40.153 --> 00:35:42.070
Giving a model of what
we should be doing down

00:35:42.070 --> 00:35:44.320
the Avenue with
landscape and crosswalks

00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:49.660
and restaurants and all this
stuff to make it more lively.

00:35:49.660 --> 00:35:51.880
You may have heard
that since it opened,

00:35:51.880 --> 00:35:54.190
the Broad has received
600,000 visitors.

00:35:54.190 --> 00:35:55.960
It's a free museum.

00:35:55.960 --> 00:35:58.390
The lines go around the block.

00:35:58.390 --> 00:36:01.750
60% of the people
claim to be non-white.

00:36:04.840 --> 00:36:08.530
The median income
is $20,000 less

00:36:08.530 --> 00:36:11.500
than the median income
in other museums.

00:36:11.500 --> 00:36:12.400
This is a survey.

00:36:12.400 --> 00:36:14.140
You can ask me the
statistics later.

00:36:14.140 --> 00:36:16.180
I'm not exactly sure
where that came from.

00:36:16.180 --> 00:36:21.135
But essentially, the freeness
of this museum has exposed--

00:36:21.135 --> 00:36:22.510
75% of the people
say they really

00:36:22.510 --> 00:36:25.510
have not been exposed
to contemporary art.

00:36:25.510 --> 00:36:27.790
That is fairly phenomenal.

00:36:27.790 --> 00:36:30.415
That Eli Broad, a person who has
been vilified in Los Angeles--

00:36:30.415 --> 00:36:31.082
[ALARM GOES OFF]

00:36:31.082 --> 00:36:32.350
I do have three minutes extra.

00:36:32.350 --> 00:36:34.870
I'm almost done.

00:36:34.870 --> 00:36:37.360
Just two days ago, it was
on Jeopardy as a category.

00:36:37.360 --> 00:36:40.264
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:41.720 --> 00:36:44.848
Two museums that are underway
with two simple ideas

00:36:44.848 --> 00:36:45.890
that we could talk about.

00:36:45.890 --> 00:36:48.230
Inclusivity, and it
goes to the point

00:36:48.230 --> 00:36:49.490
I just made about the Broad.

00:36:49.490 --> 00:36:51.980
How can we take the
haves and have nots?

00:36:51.980 --> 00:36:55.430
How can we bring down the museum
so that it's not a barrier?

00:36:55.430 --> 00:36:59.210
In Rio de Janeiro, right
on Copacabana beach,

00:36:59.210 --> 00:37:02.180
a Democratic surface and
one block away from one

00:37:02.180 --> 00:37:06.290
of the most famous favelas,
completely different in nature,

00:37:06.290 --> 00:37:09.170
we were commissioned to do
the Museum of Image and Sound,

00:37:09.170 --> 00:37:12.560
a museum dedicated
to Brazilian artists.

00:37:12.560 --> 00:37:15.170
We wanted to also,
like the ICA, build

00:37:15.170 --> 00:37:17.150
this out of the
seeds of the city.

00:37:17.150 --> 00:37:18.920
It's a museum about Rio.

00:37:18.920 --> 00:37:20.360
We wanted to make it from Rio.

00:37:20.360 --> 00:37:22.430
We happened to be on
this beautiful sidewalk

00:37:22.430 --> 00:37:26.630
by Burle Marx and we basically
pulled the museum out

00:37:26.630 --> 00:37:29.390
of that sidewalk.

00:37:29.390 --> 00:37:30.590
That's what it looks like.

00:37:30.590 --> 00:37:32.150
The most important
thing about this

00:37:32.150 --> 00:37:34.310
is we made it so that
you could walk up

00:37:34.310 --> 00:37:36.710
the building, all the way
to a roof, free of charge

00:37:36.710 --> 00:37:37.490
without a ticket.

00:37:40.370 --> 00:37:42.380
This is the inside
of the museum.

00:37:42.380 --> 00:37:44.900
It's an interpretive museum
and a fine art museum,

00:37:44.900 --> 00:37:45.950
both at the same time.

00:37:45.950 --> 00:37:47.810
That's another thing
we want to think about.

00:37:47.810 --> 00:37:50.185
I know Dave has been doing a
lot of interpretive museums,

00:37:50.185 --> 00:37:51.140
so have we.

00:37:51.140 --> 00:37:54.140
The top is a rooftop cinema,
restaurant, and park.

00:37:54.140 --> 00:37:55.760
It's a green space.

00:37:55.760 --> 00:37:58.640
No one can get to a
rooftop on Copacabana Beach

00:37:58.640 --> 00:38:02.630
unless you're a hotel guest
or a member of a private club.

00:38:02.630 --> 00:38:04.800
This is about democracy.

00:38:04.800 --> 00:38:05.300
Here it is.

00:38:05.300 --> 00:38:08.010
It's under construction,
it's almost finished.

00:38:08.010 --> 00:38:11.300
However, the economic downturn--
oh, and there are little--

00:38:11.300 --> 00:38:16.028
these cobogó focus your view to
the view of Copacabana Beach,

00:38:16.028 --> 00:38:18.320
which is, of course, one of
the most famous beach views

00:38:18.320 --> 00:38:19.010
in the world.

00:38:19.010 --> 00:38:20.600
Last thing I'm
going to talk about,

00:38:20.600 --> 00:38:23.540
and I'll be done in five
slides, flexibility.

00:38:23.540 --> 00:38:24.410
What does it mean?

00:38:24.410 --> 00:38:31.280
Scale, medium, light, dark,
gray box, white box, black box.

00:38:31.280 --> 00:38:32.960
What does that mean these days?

00:38:32.960 --> 00:38:35.570
How do we anticipate
the art of the future

00:38:35.570 --> 00:38:37.110
and what makes art anyway?

00:38:37.110 --> 00:38:38.510
Is it performance?

00:38:38.510 --> 00:38:39.890
Is it food?

00:38:39.890 --> 00:38:43.100
Is it car design?

00:38:43.100 --> 00:38:47.300
We were tasked with thinking
of a new cultural institution.

00:38:47.300 --> 00:38:48.920
We call it Culture Shed.

00:38:48.920 --> 00:38:50.450
It's a museum that
can change shape.

00:38:50.450 --> 00:38:52.100
It's a shape shifter.

00:38:52.100 --> 00:38:54.980
It's got a huge
volume that slides out

00:38:54.980 --> 00:39:00.980
from over a set of fixed
floored museum quality spaces.

00:39:00.980 --> 00:39:04.760
This is 130-foot cube.

00:39:04.760 --> 00:39:06.770
It allows all kinds
of programming

00:39:06.770 --> 00:39:09.860
to happen simultaneously.

00:39:09.860 --> 00:39:12.710
The element that moves out can
be moved out in 15 minutes.

00:39:12.710 --> 00:39:13.610
Column-free.

00:39:13.610 --> 00:39:18.050
Floor plates can be used
for art exhibition, theater.

00:39:18.050 --> 00:39:20.070
Complete flexibility,
completely opposite

00:39:20.070 --> 00:39:22.950
of what David was
talking about at Denver.

00:39:22.950 --> 00:39:24.980
Here's what it
looks like and it's

00:39:24.980 --> 00:39:26.310
going to open in three years.

00:39:26.310 --> 00:39:27.249
Thank you.

00:39:27.249 --> 00:39:30.616
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:30.616 --> 00:39:31.241
That was great.

00:39:31.241 --> 00:39:35.233
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:40.710 --> 00:39:41.210
Oh.

00:39:52.128 --> 00:39:52.670
That's great.

00:39:56.920 --> 00:39:57.720
Do we wear these?

00:40:01.496 --> 00:40:02.912
[LAUGHING]

00:40:07.530 --> 00:40:09.240
Lost it.

00:40:09.240 --> 00:40:09.740
Thank you.

00:40:20.840 --> 00:40:22.010
We decided to--

00:40:22.010 --> 00:40:22.640
Change it?

00:40:22.640 --> 00:40:27.530
--change it up a little because
the three women thought that

00:40:27.530 --> 00:40:31.070
instead of standing up
individually with our responses

00:40:31.070 --> 00:40:35.440
to the presentations we had
not yet seen, we would--

00:40:35.440 --> 00:40:35.940
[LAUGHING]

00:40:35.940 --> 00:40:39.110
--in fact, open up a
conversation as we are

00:40:39.110 --> 00:40:39.740
want to do.

00:40:39.740 --> 00:40:41.000
That's right.

00:40:41.000 --> 00:40:44.180
And respond conversationally.

00:40:44.180 --> 00:40:45.230
That's right.

00:40:45.230 --> 00:40:46.790
We unanimously
made that decision.

00:40:46.790 --> 00:40:47.310
We did.

00:40:47.310 --> 00:40:49.062
[LAUGHING]

00:40:49.062 --> 00:40:49.940
There we go.

00:40:49.940 --> 00:40:50.660
And here we are.

00:40:50.660 --> 00:40:51.350
And here we are.

00:40:54.645 --> 00:40:55.770
Jill, do you want to start?

00:40:55.770 --> 00:40:57.230
I'll start.

00:40:57.230 --> 00:40:59.420
First of all, it's a
great pleasure to be here

00:40:59.420 --> 00:41:04.250
and to be part of your
McDermott Award residency.

00:41:04.250 --> 00:41:06.320
And to be here, kind
of the hometown girl

00:41:06.320 --> 00:41:09.290
so I like that a lot.

00:41:09.290 --> 00:41:12.440
It's very interesting and I
have not had this experience

00:41:12.440 --> 00:41:16.040
in a really long time, of
hearing one of our architects

00:41:16.040 --> 00:41:18.320
talk about our building.

00:41:18.320 --> 00:41:21.470
Because I both see it
differently and I certainly

00:41:21.470 --> 00:41:25.730
experience it differently and I
didn't design it and then leave

00:41:25.730 --> 00:41:27.020
it.

00:41:27.020 --> 00:41:29.360
Though we all stayed close.

00:41:29.360 --> 00:41:32.240
I actually animated
and activated.

00:41:32.240 --> 00:41:36.200
I thought to myself, as a
starting point, that in so much

00:41:36.200 --> 00:41:40.730
of your language and your
architectural vocabulary,

00:41:40.730 --> 00:41:42.740
you talk a lot
about the piercing

00:41:42.740 --> 00:41:44.690
and the disorientation.

00:41:44.690 --> 00:41:47.210
It made me remember
specifically why

00:41:47.210 --> 00:41:48.530
I think we were such a great--

00:41:48.530 --> 00:41:50.120
Why we are such a great pair.

00:41:50.120 --> 00:41:53.060
Because you were looking
for that discomfort

00:41:53.060 --> 00:41:55.430
and disorientation
and I was looking

00:41:55.430 --> 00:41:57.680
for affection and attachment.

00:41:57.680 --> 00:42:01.070
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:01.070 --> 00:42:03.080
I wanted to build
that into the building

00:42:03.080 --> 00:42:05.420
and to make it intimate
and to create a place

00:42:05.420 --> 00:42:08.120
that people would come to love.

00:42:08.120 --> 00:42:11.090
And that it would be loved by
many different kinds of people

00:42:11.090 --> 00:42:14.360
and many different
generations of people.

00:42:14.360 --> 00:42:17.180
And that it would be
a place of belonging

00:42:17.180 --> 00:42:21.170
in a city that had never had
a place for contemporary art

00:42:21.170 --> 00:42:24.020
and contemporary ideas.

00:42:24.020 --> 00:42:28.160
That was one of my
first responses is that.

00:42:28.160 --> 00:42:30.830
I think that the
beauty of the ICA

00:42:30.830 --> 00:42:33.500
comes in fact from
these different pushes

00:42:33.500 --> 00:42:37.520
and pulls that we were
able to do together.

00:42:37.520 --> 00:42:39.590
I think also that I
thought about very

00:42:39.590 --> 00:42:42.530
much the transparency
of our building.

00:42:42.530 --> 00:42:45.920
Now, in the seaport around the
ICA, I'm really sick of glass.

00:42:45.920 --> 00:42:49.880
I'm not sick of you glass
but the transparency

00:42:49.880 --> 00:42:53.270
was something that was both
something that was material,

00:42:53.270 --> 00:42:56.390
the materiality of it,
but also philosophically,

00:42:56.390 --> 00:42:58.820
we were interested
in the transparency.

00:42:58.820 --> 00:43:02.300
It mattered to us
and to the ICA board

00:43:02.300 --> 00:43:04.940
tremendously, that when you
walk in, the first thing you see

00:43:04.940 --> 00:43:06.650
is our education spaces.

00:43:06.650 --> 00:43:09.080
So that our kids
and our families

00:43:09.080 --> 00:43:12.740
are seen as one of the core
constituencies in our museum,

00:43:12.740 --> 00:43:15.240
alongside with our
public and our donors.

00:43:15.240 --> 00:43:20.270
Our teams, which is, to my mind,
the museum of the future to me,

00:43:20.270 --> 00:43:24.740
is the teams at the ICA and
the teams at the Studio Museum.

00:43:24.740 --> 00:43:27.350
And the team sitting in
this audience tonight,

00:43:27.350 --> 00:43:28.730
thank you for coming.

00:43:28.730 --> 00:43:31.220
But that they are
in our boardrooms.

00:43:31.220 --> 00:43:32.570
There they are with our board.

00:43:32.570 --> 00:43:36.590
They are equal to our board
of trustees in the importance

00:43:36.590 --> 00:43:39.290
and the vision and the
commitment that they bring.

00:43:39.290 --> 00:43:44.240
These are the things
that I was provoked at.

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:47.600
I do think that the questions
that I'm so interested to learn

00:43:47.600 --> 00:43:53.810
from are how we talk about this
discourse or this conversation

00:43:53.810 --> 00:43:55.730
between the past and
the present, the present

00:43:55.730 --> 00:43:58.460
and the future, between
artists and audiences,

00:43:58.460 --> 00:43:59.780
between artists and objects.

00:44:03.080 --> 00:44:05.600
Again, between
middle aged people

00:44:05.600 --> 00:44:11.030
like myself and young people,
between scholars and students.

00:44:11.030 --> 00:44:15.170
I keep seeing it as setting up
a possibility of a discourse.

00:44:15.170 --> 00:44:19.430
I know I bristle
when we talk about,

00:44:19.430 --> 00:44:22.650
as all the architects do,
the wall wants to move.

00:44:22.650 --> 00:44:25.640
You had a wall that wanted
to move or a facade.

00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:27.620
And you have many walls
that want to move.

00:44:27.620 --> 00:44:29.540
But I was like, what do we want?

00:44:32.240 --> 00:44:35.750
To basically create
a place for what

00:44:35.750 --> 00:44:37.940
could be possible
because none of it's

00:44:37.940 --> 00:44:41.390
possible without people.

00:44:41.390 --> 00:44:44.420
I know that, this is I think
the last thing I'll say and then

00:44:44.420 --> 00:44:46.580
turn it over to
Lorna and Thelma.

00:44:46.580 --> 00:44:49.160
But I know for me, when
we built the new museum--

00:44:49.160 --> 00:44:50.960
It was a big deal to
build this museum.

00:44:50.960 --> 00:44:55.160
It was an against all odds
story in the city that

00:44:55.160 --> 00:44:58.040
did not value contemporary
art tremendously

00:44:58.040 --> 00:45:02.480
and not collected it in any
way, did not have much of.

00:45:02.480 --> 00:45:05.000
I still think we lack
a strong ecosystem

00:45:05.000 --> 00:45:08.420
but now, we have a
greatly changed ecosystem

00:45:08.420 --> 00:45:12.170
for the art of our time
and the ideas of our time.

00:45:12.170 --> 00:45:15.050
But what I thought
about most was, OK, now

00:45:15.050 --> 00:45:16.550
we're going to build
this new museum

00:45:16.550 --> 00:45:17.870
and raise all this money.

00:45:17.870 --> 00:45:21.830
What resources does this
allow us to put in service

00:45:21.830 --> 00:45:24.110
to our community?

00:45:24.110 --> 00:45:27.290
In addition to art,
artists, and ideas,

00:45:27.290 --> 00:45:30.080
which are our bread and
butter, the two things

00:45:30.080 --> 00:45:33.410
that I thought the most about
were access to the waterfront,

00:45:33.410 --> 00:45:36.050
because access to
the Boston Harbor

00:45:36.050 --> 00:45:39.740
was pretty nonexistent
in a programmed way,

00:45:39.740 --> 00:45:42.200
in a beautiful way.

00:45:42.200 --> 00:45:45.290
Similarly to what you're
describing in Rio.

00:45:45.290 --> 00:45:49.130
You need to be wealthy to
have a waterfront apartment.

00:45:51.740 --> 00:45:54.350
At the time we were
building the museum,

00:45:54.350 --> 00:45:59.570
there was a tremendous need for
programming in the after school

00:45:59.570 --> 00:46:02.390
hours between 2:00 and
7:00 for our teams,

00:46:02.390 --> 00:46:05.810
where we knew that in Boston,
our Boston teams needed

00:46:05.810 --> 00:46:07.880
more support, more activity.

00:46:07.880 --> 00:46:09.830
They needed a
community to be part

00:46:09.830 --> 00:46:12.200
of their education writ large.

00:46:12.200 --> 00:46:14.600
That the true education
of our young people

00:46:14.600 --> 00:46:17.810
requires our community-based
institutions, which

00:46:17.810 --> 00:46:19.970
I consider a museum to be one.

00:46:19.970 --> 00:46:24.200
So we invested in teens.

00:46:24.200 --> 00:46:25.300
We have the best ones.

00:46:28.580 --> 00:46:31.490
For those of us who
have raised teens,

00:46:31.490 --> 00:46:33.740
you know how they
keep you young.

00:46:33.740 --> 00:46:36.590
But more than that,
they keep you honest.

00:46:36.590 --> 00:46:38.600
When we think about
the issues of our city,

00:46:38.600 --> 00:46:42.410
even though now we're
challenged by being, again,

00:46:42.410 --> 00:46:44.930
in a privatized and
wealthy enclave,

00:46:44.930 --> 00:46:48.650
because that's what the
ICA has partly engendered

00:46:48.650 --> 00:46:51.800
in the value of that real estate
and that's a troubling thing

00:46:51.800 --> 00:46:53.450
I'd love to talk about.

00:46:53.450 --> 00:46:56.030
The gentrification that
is accompanying so much

00:46:56.030 --> 00:46:58.340
of the museum construction.

00:46:58.340 --> 00:47:01.790
But we are not removed
from our young people.

00:47:01.790 --> 00:47:05.690
Our young people
keep us connected.

00:47:05.690 --> 00:47:09.560
However much we talk about the
work we do teaching our teens,

00:47:09.560 --> 00:47:12.890
I think the greater truth
is that they have changed us

00:47:12.890 --> 00:47:15.290
more than we have changed them.

00:47:15.290 --> 00:47:18.500
That's my initial response.

00:47:18.500 --> 00:47:20.690
Thank you.

00:47:20.690 --> 00:47:21.950
It's great to be here.

00:47:21.950 --> 00:47:26.000
David and I are at the beginning
of what Charles and Jill are

00:47:26.000 --> 00:47:28.370
at the other end of, right?

00:47:28.370 --> 00:47:33.260
I have to defend my decision
for the lack of images.

00:47:33.260 --> 00:47:36.440
It really is because we are
at the very, very, very, very

00:47:36.440 --> 00:47:41.690
beginning of designing what will
be the Studio Museum in Harlem.

00:47:41.690 --> 00:47:43.640
In many ways, this panel
is about the future

00:47:43.640 --> 00:47:46.400
of the museum but our--

00:47:46.400 --> 00:47:49.430
Can we get the slide of the
future Studio Museum in Harlem?

00:47:49.430 --> 00:47:50.360
Look at what we have.

00:47:50.360 --> 00:47:51.510
Oh, you have your--

00:47:51.510 --> 00:47:52.490
Well, we have--

00:47:52.490 --> 00:47:54.710
Yeah, OK.

00:47:54.710 --> 00:47:57.140
In thinking about the
future of the Studio Museum,

00:47:57.140 --> 00:47:59.990
it really comes from
the issues of our past.

00:47:59.990 --> 00:48:01.880
And that is, that
the Studio Museum

00:48:01.880 --> 00:48:05.390
was founded in 1968 on a
real question and a belief.

00:48:05.390 --> 00:48:08.570
A question about whether
museums could and would

00:48:08.570 --> 00:48:12.390
ever change to embrace
the work of Black artists.

00:48:12.390 --> 00:48:15.680
But a real belief in the power
of the work of Black artists.

00:48:15.680 --> 00:48:19.460
Therefore, the need for a museum
that would be devoted to them.

00:48:19.460 --> 00:48:24.080
In a rented loft space in Harlem
on 5th Avenue, 125th Street,

00:48:24.080 --> 00:48:29.180
we were founded but with no
idea of what our future would

00:48:29.180 --> 00:48:33.230
be, other than those
very deep founding ideals

00:48:33.230 --> 00:48:36.680
about the necessity for a
cultural institution that

00:48:36.680 --> 00:48:38.760
would operate deeply
in the community,

00:48:38.760 --> 00:48:42.020
but also very broadly
in the world around

00:48:42.020 --> 00:48:43.550
and about Black artists.

00:48:43.550 --> 00:48:47.420
We acquired the building
that we are currently in 1979

00:48:47.420 --> 00:48:49.490
and moved in 1981.

00:48:49.490 --> 00:48:51.290
But it was
100-year-old building,

00:48:51.290 --> 00:48:53.990
built in 1914 as a bank.

00:48:53.990 --> 00:48:57.660
And had been a bank on 125th
Street for many, many years

00:48:57.660 --> 00:49:02.210
but vacant for the 10 years
before we moved into it.

00:49:02.210 --> 00:49:04.910
When thinking about our
future, it became very clear

00:49:04.910 --> 00:49:06.770
there was a necessity
in our future

00:49:06.770 --> 00:49:09.290
just to think about the
very practical issue that

00:49:09.290 --> 00:49:14.630
came from living in as an arts
institution and a building that

00:49:14.630 --> 00:49:16.310
had been beautifully adapted.

00:49:16.310 --> 00:49:18.650
Amazing example
of adaptive reuse

00:49:18.650 --> 00:49:21.110
by the pioneering
architect Max Bond,

00:49:21.110 --> 00:49:23.390
who was a founder of
the Museum and really

00:49:23.390 --> 00:49:26.750
worked deeply to create out
of that space, a space that

00:49:26.750 --> 00:49:29.660
would encompass our studio
program, our collection,

00:49:29.660 --> 00:49:32.900
and our exhibition programs.

00:49:32.900 --> 00:49:38.240
Fast forward into 2003, '04,
'05, '06, beginning to imagine

00:49:38.240 --> 00:49:41.930
what the museum was and what
Harlem was becoming and looking

00:49:41.930 --> 00:49:45.440
towards our 50th anniversary,
it became clear we had to think

00:49:45.440 --> 00:49:46.812
about this differently.

00:49:46.812 --> 00:49:48.770
In many ways, the beginning
of the conversation

00:49:48.770 --> 00:49:52.010
that has led to what you
have seen with David and I

00:49:52.010 --> 00:49:54.200
was a brief that
was very practical

00:49:54.200 --> 00:49:57.500
because we needed everything
in a very practical way.

00:49:57.500 --> 00:50:00.890
Studio Museum has no doors
that are over 10 feet high.

00:50:00.890 --> 00:50:02.600
Every work of art
bigger than that gets

00:50:02.600 --> 00:50:04.760
unrolled out on 125th Street.

00:50:04.760 --> 00:50:06.860
We don't have a loading dock.

00:50:06.860 --> 00:50:10.370
I could go on for all of the
things that we did not have.

00:50:10.370 --> 00:50:12.440
But really, in some
ways, because I'm

00:50:12.440 --> 00:50:14.810
a curator who now is
a museum director,

00:50:14.810 --> 00:50:18.620
the brief started as
an exhibition query.

00:50:18.620 --> 00:50:21.890
What I said to
David and his team

00:50:21.890 --> 00:50:25.040
is that I wanted a
building that knowing

00:50:25.040 --> 00:50:27.470
what our founding mission
was and was about.

00:50:27.470 --> 00:50:29.810
In a way, I would say the
founders of the Studio Museum

00:50:29.810 --> 00:50:31.370
put our mission into our name.

00:50:31.370 --> 00:50:32.900
Studio Museum Harlem.

00:50:32.900 --> 00:50:36.560
The three critical
factors of what we are.

00:50:36.560 --> 00:50:40.700
I said I wanted also
in this new building

00:50:40.700 --> 00:50:44.030
to evidence the three
experiences of Harlem

00:50:44.030 --> 00:50:47.370
that I thought were
incredibly important.

00:50:47.370 --> 00:50:49.430
The first was the
experience of the street

00:50:49.430 --> 00:50:51.980
and understanding the
street life of Harlem

00:50:51.980 --> 00:50:55.020
as being the way in which
Harlem understands itself.

00:50:55.020 --> 00:50:58.320
I wanted the experience
of the stage and the idea

00:50:58.320 --> 00:51:00.983
that for many the
idea of Black culture

00:51:00.983 --> 00:51:02.400
comes through the
performing arts.

00:51:02.400 --> 00:51:04.483
But in Harlem, that has
been known from the Harlem

00:51:04.483 --> 00:51:07.380
Renaissance to the present
through the great performing

00:51:07.380 --> 00:51:09.000
arts culture that
plays out formally

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:10.920
and informally through
the neighborhood.

00:51:10.920 --> 00:51:13.890
Then finally, I wanted the
experience of the sanctuary.

00:51:13.890 --> 00:51:16.110
Important, critical experience.

00:51:16.110 --> 00:51:19.950
Both the physical space of the
sanctuary as the civic space,

00:51:19.950 --> 00:51:23.010
as a spiritual space, as a
political space, but also

00:51:23.010 --> 00:51:25.080
the way in which
those spaces in Harlem

00:51:25.080 --> 00:51:26.640
have punctuated
the neighborhood.

00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:32.490
Creating harbors and
houses of all faiths.

00:51:32.490 --> 00:51:34.560
David responded
to that by adding

00:51:34.560 --> 00:51:38.180
one more, which was the
stoop, which in many ways

00:51:38.180 --> 00:51:39.180
brought it all together.

00:51:39.180 --> 00:51:40.860
Because with the
stoop, what David did

00:51:40.860 --> 00:51:45.990
is spoke very beautifully
about this space in Harlem

00:51:45.990 --> 00:51:48.720
that connects the public
space and the private space.

00:51:48.720 --> 00:51:50.468
This interstitial space.

00:51:50.468 --> 00:51:52.260
Coming out of your
house to be on the stoop

00:51:52.260 --> 00:51:54.060
or coming off the street.

00:51:54.060 --> 00:51:56.760
The way in which the stoop
was a gathering place.

00:51:56.760 --> 00:51:58.950
That's really what
we've been working with.

00:51:58.950 --> 00:52:01.050
Putting those ideas--

00:52:01.050 --> 00:52:03.360
The street, the stage, the
sanctuary, and the stoop.

00:52:03.360 --> 00:52:06.420
--up against the loading
dock, some good HVAC--

00:52:06.420 --> 00:52:07.530
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:07.530 --> 00:52:11.760
--some flexible walls, a little
pour of concrete on the floor.

00:52:11.760 --> 00:52:13.558
See, this is now
a design meeting.

00:52:13.558 --> 00:52:14.850
Pour of concrete on the floors.

00:52:14.850 --> 00:52:17.010
But putting that
together to create what

00:52:17.010 --> 00:52:20.640
would be in a neighborhood
that is radically changing.

00:52:20.640 --> 00:52:22.740
Gentrification is
one part of it.

00:52:22.740 --> 00:52:26.400
But it's also changing in
its architectural frame.

00:52:26.400 --> 00:52:29.760
What David, he spoke about this
beautifully and has so much,

00:52:29.760 --> 00:52:33.480
puncturing what is becoming
a commercial architecture

00:52:33.480 --> 00:52:35.700
of the neighborhood
of 125th Street

00:52:35.700 --> 00:52:38.940
with what will be this building,
this cultural anchor, one

00:52:38.940 --> 00:52:40.680
block west of the Apollo.

00:52:40.680 --> 00:52:43.380
Sitting, as we think,
looking across at each other,

00:52:43.380 --> 00:52:46.200
really evidencing
for the neighborhood,

00:52:46.200 --> 00:52:47.640
the immediate
community, the idea

00:52:47.640 --> 00:52:50.370
of what it means to
have a cultural anchor

00:52:50.370 --> 00:52:51.600
in the neighborhood.

00:52:51.600 --> 00:52:57.420
We are in schematic design
and thinking about making

00:52:57.420 --> 00:52:58.490
all of these things real.

00:52:58.490 --> 00:52:59.353
Are you laughing?

00:52:59.353 --> 00:53:00.020
No, we're like--

00:53:00.020 --> 00:53:01.080
Reminiscing.

00:53:01.080 --> 00:53:02.220
We're reminiscing?

00:53:02.220 --> 00:53:04.890
Yeah, we're nostalgic
for something

00:53:04.890 --> 00:53:06.870
but It's not schematic design--

00:53:06.870 --> 00:53:07.830
Storage.

00:53:07.830 --> 00:53:08.610
--at this point.

00:53:08.610 --> 00:53:11.670
[LAUGHTER]

00:53:11.670 --> 00:53:12.510
Yeah.

00:53:12.510 --> 00:53:14.580
But we are in this process.

00:53:14.580 --> 00:53:16.140
What I'll say about
the process is

00:53:16.140 --> 00:53:18.870
it's been made amazing
for us as an institution

00:53:18.870 --> 00:53:22.470
because David has responded so
profoundly and so powerfully

00:53:22.470 --> 00:53:26.130
not just to our needs
but to our aspirations.

00:53:26.130 --> 00:53:30.450
That's what has made it, even
in this beginning moment, such

00:53:30.450 --> 00:53:34.740
an amazing process where we can
see what the Studio Museum will

00:53:34.740 --> 00:53:38.190
be, which in so many ways,
is what our founders imagined

00:53:38.190 --> 00:53:40.350
in 1968.

00:53:40.350 --> 00:53:43.380
They really saw that
possibility and David has made

00:53:43.380 --> 00:53:44.790
it manifest in this design.

00:53:49.120 --> 00:53:51.550
I come to this as the
artist and as someone

00:53:51.550 --> 00:53:53.710
who is offered to
have exhibitions

00:53:53.710 --> 00:53:58.520
or to speak in museums,
engage the collection,

00:53:58.520 --> 00:54:02.920
do part of educational programs
of different institutions.

00:54:02.920 --> 00:54:06.670
I would say over the course
of my career, that's varied,

00:54:06.670 --> 00:54:09.980
even in terms of the reception
of my work in [INAUDIBLE],, had

00:54:09.980 --> 00:54:11.980
lectures maybe in the
'80s where there were bomb

00:54:11.980 --> 00:54:14.350
threats for just
having an artist who's

00:54:14.350 --> 00:54:16.720
doing a certain kind of work.

00:54:16.720 --> 00:54:20.320
Lecture at a hall and
having to talk to security

00:54:20.320 --> 00:54:22.180
about how I proceed.

00:54:22.180 --> 00:54:25.930
My experience goes, in terms
of exhibiting in the United

00:54:25.930 --> 00:54:28.400
States, quite broadly.

00:54:28.400 --> 00:54:30.190
But what I've come to--

00:54:30.190 --> 00:54:32.080
It's not so much
about the buildings.

00:54:32.080 --> 00:54:34.630
I mean, yes, we can
all point to Bilbao

00:54:34.630 --> 00:54:38.908
and the Guggenheim as this
franchise, that just gets

00:54:38.908 --> 00:54:40.450
put out through
different governments

00:54:40.450 --> 00:54:43.450
and built in any kind of
fashion that becomes necessary.

00:54:43.450 --> 00:54:46.990
But I think the commitment,
as you speak of United States

00:54:46.990 --> 00:54:50.170
and as some of the projects
in Rivington Place that David

00:54:50.170 --> 00:54:54.310
talks about, in terms of
mandate of knowing where you are

00:54:54.310 --> 00:54:57.880
and your relationship to a
community and having a mandate

00:54:57.880 --> 00:55:00.250
and how that gets served.

00:55:00.250 --> 00:55:03.670
Yes, I think over the past,
I've been doing art for a while

00:55:03.670 --> 00:55:06.230
but I would say, in my
career in the past 20 years,

00:55:06.230 --> 00:55:08.800
I've seen a lot of
commitment really to,

00:55:08.800 --> 00:55:12.340
how does a museum
shift, change, expand,

00:55:12.340 --> 00:55:14.440
and still have the
money to do programming?

00:55:14.440 --> 00:55:17.050
To still engage its community?

00:55:17.050 --> 00:55:20.260
Community really meaning
young people, families, people

00:55:20.260 --> 00:55:22.870
who may not be able to afford.

00:55:22.870 --> 00:55:24.470
How do you get them
into the building?

00:55:24.470 --> 00:55:30.100
How do you broaden the economic
and racial and ethnic scope

00:55:30.100 --> 00:55:31.690
of who your audience
is or who you

00:55:31.690 --> 00:55:33.100
think your audience could be?

00:55:33.100 --> 00:55:36.910
And how that should shift
and change and broaden.

00:55:36.910 --> 00:55:39.860
On the other side, which doesn't
in terms of the art world,

00:55:39.860 --> 00:55:44.050
and only maybe in this past year
or so getting a little bit more

00:55:44.050 --> 00:55:46.840
discussed, is inside the museum.

00:55:46.840 --> 00:55:48.910
As an artist,
again, my experience

00:55:48.910 --> 00:55:52.600
in terms of curatorial
staff, educational staff,

00:55:52.600 --> 00:55:55.420
the development department,
if I, as an artist,

00:55:55.420 --> 00:55:57.790
walk into any of
these rooms that

00:55:57.790 --> 00:56:03.070
are behind the scenes, many of
the times everyone is white.

00:56:03.070 --> 00:56:06.310
Not all the time and
not in all institutions

00:56:06.310 --> 00:56:09.970
but in many major institutions
throughout the country.

00:56:09.970 --> 00:56:13.480
While we have this idea
of place and where we are

00:56:13.480 --> 00:56:16.690
and how we're serving this
other community that's outside

00:56:16.690 --> 00:56:19.300
or needs to be
brought in, the mirror

00:56:19.300 --> 00:56:23.065
also has to be turned around
to the interior of construction

00:56:23.065 --> 00:56:25.420
of who is working in the
museum and what level

00:56:25.420 --> 00:56:30.670
and how diverse and broad
and rich is that experience.

00:56:30.670 --> 00:56:34.810
Not to say that the
diversity of one's staff

00:56:34.810 --> 00:56:39.430
points to expertise because the
relationship to what people do

00:56:39.430 --> 00:56:42.640
and what their focus is
does not necessarily have

00:56:42.640 --> 00:56:44.260
to do with their ethnicity.

00:56:44.260 --> 00:56:46.930
But this broad sense
of, why does it always

00:56:46.930 --> 00:56:49.450
end up so homogeneously?

00:56:49.450 --> 00:56:53.140
And the stakes for
keeping it homogeneously

00:56:53.140 --> 00:56:55.270
is a big question to me.

00:56:55.270 --> 00:56:58.480
While there are all these
different programs--

00:56:58.480 --> 00:57:01.570
To be honest, in watching
Thelma at the Studio Museum

00:57:01.570 --> 00:57:05.620
and the mandate of the Studio
Museum, which has been amazing,

00:57:05.620 --> 00:57:07.630
and watching not
only the development

00:57:07.630 --> 00:57:11.380
of the career of artists and
their work through the studio

00:57:11.380 --> 00:57:16.450
program, but also developing
the careers of young curators.

00:57:16.450 --> 00:57:20.410
Where they get the opportunity
straight out of graduate school

00:57:20.410 --> 00:57:23.650
to act as curators,
who get immediately

00:57:23.650 --> 00:57:27.220
hired away by other
large institutions.

00:57:27.220 --> 00:57:29.900
But that's also
part of the point.

00:57:29.900 --> 00:57:32.140
But it seems like no
one else can find a way

00:57:32.140 --> 00:57:35.500
to find those graduate
students who are working

00:57:35.500 --> 00:57:37.960
and that there is a broad
collection of people.

00:57:37.960 --> 00:57:43.060
In that way, that's my
other hope for the future.

00:57:43.060 --> 00:57:46.390
That in terms of not only
the way that the mandate

00:57:46.390 --> 00:57:48.400
of the museum in terms
of how they're built

00:57:48.400 --> 00:57:51.850
and how they want to outreach,
but also their inclusiveness

00:57:51.850 --> 00:57:54.370
inside as well.

00:57:54.370 --> 00:57:56.470
Thank you.

00:57:56.470 --> 00:57:59.590
Thank you all very
much for the responses,

00:57:59.590 --> 00:58:02.080
comments, speculations.

00:58:02.080 --> 00:58:05.630
We'll have many, many
threads to pull, I think,

00:58:05.630 --> 00:58:08.440
at this point in
terms of conversation.

00:58:08.440 --> 00:58:10.660
We'll just try to proceed
with a discussion,

00:58:10.660 --> 00:58:14.080
leaving a little bit of time
at the end for some questions

00:58:14.080 --> 00:58:16.330
from the audience as well.

00:58:16.330 --> 00:58:18.310
What I like to do, I
think just to prompt

00:58:18.310 --> 00:58:22.480
some initial elaborations
from each of you

00:58:22.480 --> 00:58:28.390
is to ask you to develop a
bit the issue of audience

00:58:28.390 --> 00:58:30.880
that has come up in the
different presentations.

00:58:30.880 --> 00:58:33.620
But I want to frame
that in a particular way

00:58:33.620 --> 00:58:35.920
to try to associate
it with certain terms

00:58:35.920 --> 00:58:39.190
that I've heard you
using respectively.

00:58:39.190 --> 00:58:41.290
I'd like to frame the
question of audience

00:58:41.290 --> 00:58:45.010
around the need for the museum
or the desire for the museum

00:58:45.010 --> 00:58:49.030
to calibrate wonder
and familiarity.

00:58:49.030 --> 00:58:52.480
I think wonder is a
word I put forward to,

00:58:52.480 --> 00:58:54.670
in part, because it's
the source of the museum.

00:58:54.670 --> 00:58:57.050
The cabinet of wonders
right from centuries ago

00:58:57.050 --> 00:58:58.990
and from these initial
moments of seeing

00:58:58.990 --> 00:59:03.670
the museum as a place apart,
a place of seeing the unknown,

00:59:03.670 --> 00:59:06.610
seeing the unimaginable,
and so forth.

00:59:06.610 --> 00:59:09.063
That it has continued
as a space of wonder

00:59:09.063 --> 00:59:10.480
that we now might
use other words,

00:59:10.480 --> 00:59:13.210
like spectacle,
which has come up.

00:59:13.210 --> 00:59:17.380
Then on the other side of this,
the other term, familiarity.

00:59:17.380 --> 00:59:19.690
I put forward because
I think we also

00:59:19.690 --> 00:59:23.770
have a sense that over
the past century or so,

00:59:23.770 --> 00:59:26.020
two things have happened
with regard to museum.

00:59:26.020 --> 00:59:28.450
One of which is simply
that museums have

00:59:28.450 --> 00:59:29.950
become more familiar spaces.

00:59:29.950 --> 00:59:33.100
And such that we can now have
international art tourism

00:59:33.100 --> 00:59:36.670
that we know the
behaviors of movement

00:59:36.670 --> 00:59:39.130
in a museum as a
sort of expectation.

00:59:39.130 --> 00:59:40.160
That's one side of it.

00:59:40.160 --> 00:59:43.280
But I think more
importantly, you said it

00:59:43.280 --> 00:59:46.090
in several different points,
you've raised the need

00:59:46.090 --> 00:59:49.390
for the museum to be
a space of familiarity

00:59:49.390 --> 00:59:51.130
for its local audience.

00:59:51.130 --> 00:59:54.130
A space that solicits the
engagement of its community,

00:59:54.130 --> 00:59:57.070
be a space of belonging.

00:59:57.070 --> 00:59:58.930
I put forward these
two terms because it

00:59:58.930 --> 01:00:01.960
seems to me that they're not
immediately reconcilable.

01:00:01.960 --> 01:00:05.290
To produce wonder, to
produce the sense of a place

01:00:05.290 --> 01:00:08.230
apart and a specialness,
seems, in some ways,

01:00:08.230 --> 01:00:10.870
to work against the
desire to produce

01:00:10.870 --> 01:00:13.450
a space that is recognizable.

01:00:13.450 --> 01:00:15.520
A space that an
audience will recognize

01:00:15.520 --> 01:00:18.580
that it has an entitlement
to, that it belongs there,

01:00:18.580 --> 01:00:21.160
that it's allowed to enter into,
and that it can participate

01:00:21.160 --> 01:00:25.660
in as part of a local
community or as part

01:00:25.660 --> 01:00:29.110
of a specific
constituency group.

01:00:29.110 --> 01:00:30.400
I put forward those two terms.

01:00:30.400 --> 01:00:32.590
I think they attach to
terms that you've used

01:00:32.590 --> 01:00:35.680
and so I won't force them
on you as a vocabulary.

01:00:35.680 --> 01:00:38.500
But I'm wondering how you,
both in terms of architecture,

01:00:38.500 --> 01:00:42.880
in terms of curation, but also
in terms of our practice, where

01:00:42.880 --> 01:00:46.180
there is a similarly a
desire to produce wonder

01:00:46.180 --> 01:00:50.080
but at the same time to
cultivate an audience's sense

01:00:50.080 --> 01:00:51.910
of expectation and familiarity.

01:00:51.910 --> 01:00:55.270
How do you think about the
calibration of those two

01:00:55.270 --> 01:00:56.590
dimensions in the museum?

01:00:56.590 --> 01:00:58.840
I think that's the
perfect question

01:00:58.840 --> 01:01:00.070
to start the conversation.

01:01:00.070 --> 01:01:01.570
Because in a way,
I think that there

01:01:01.570 --> 01:01:04.680
is the paradox about the
discourse of what the museum is

01:01:04.680 --> 01:01:06.270
and the role of the museum.

01:01:06.270 --> 01:01:12.240
From what you posit, this idea
that the wonder [INAUDIBLE],,

01:01:12.240 --> 01:01:16.380
the box of wonder, which
is made in a palace

01:01:16.380 --> 01:01:19.590
traditionally within a
community, which is completely

01:01:19.590 --> 01:01:24.750
hierarchically stratified,
or the elite and the surf,

01:01:24.750 --> 01:01:27.420
as it were, was about
a way to try and start

01:01:27.420 --> 01:01:31.530
creating moments of
inspiring that lower class to

01:01:31.530 --> 01:01:32.490
aspire to more.

01:01:32.490 --> 01:01:37.530
To create a civic, a
flattening of information.

01:01:37.530 --> 01:01:40.500
I think what you
get is that notion

01:01:40.500 --> 01:01:43.620
of the palace and
the wonder building

01:01:43.620 --> 01:01:46.230
works when the city is
at a certain formation.

01:01:46.230 --> 01:01:48.060
When you get to
this condition where

01:01:48.060 --> 01:01:50.550
you have the development of
a middle class, a flattening

01:01:50.550 --> 01:01:53.920
of that information, the way
in which the city has grown

01:01:53.920 --> 01:01:55.920
to the position that it
is, and at the beginning

01:01:55.920 --> 01:01:59.250
of the 21st century, the
explosion of urbanism,

01:01:59.250 --> 01:02:02.073
which changes the paradigm
of what the city is

01:02:02.073 --> 01:02:03.990
and the relationship
that we have to spectacle

01:02:03.990 --> 01:02:07.050
and the wonder of
the building, you

01:02:07.050 --> 01:02:10.050
get this hyper condition,
which I think Bilbao sums up,

01:02:10.050 --> 01:02:13.740
which is to create
this hyper palace.

01:02:13.740 --> 01:02:17.160
Almost entertaining
visual attractor

01:02:17.160 --> 01:02:18.985
to still keep the interest.

01:02:18.985 --> 01:02:21.360
Then I think that you have
something that's now, I think,

01:02:21.360 --> 01:02:23.820
becoming interesting, which
is this notion of trying

01:02:23.820 --> 01:02:29.910
to start to create
threads and systems which

01:02:29.910 --> 01:02:34.395
speak to a way of thinking about
how art practice is happening.

01:02:34.395 --> 01:02:36.270
Because our practice is
no longer necessarily

01:02:36.270 --> 01:02:38.100
about wonder and spectacle.

01:02:38.100 --> 01:02:42.000
But is about talking about
issues about the society

01:02:42.000 --> 01:02:43.500
that we live in,
the way in which we

01:02:43.500 --> 01:02:46.920
perceive issues, the way
in which we're consuming

01:02:46.920 --> 01:02:49.740
information, the way in which
the information is representing

01:02:49.740 --> 01:02:51.090
us and reflected out.

01:02:51.090 --> 01:02:54.870
You have this shift
that I think is now

01:02:54.870 --> 01:02:59.070
different to that
old 17th century

01:02:59.070 --> 01:03:04.260
notion of what the edification,
education, of the population

01:03:04.260 --> 01:03:04.883
is.

01:03:04.883 --> 01:03:06.300
I think that that
is the challenge

01:03:06.300 --> 01:03:09.420
right now because there is
a tendency in architecture

01:03:09.420 --> 01:03:12.990
to think that it's
always about the wonder.

01:03:12.990 --> 01:03:14.850
I think that there is
another notion, which

01:03:14.850 --> 01:03:17.280
is to accept that the
wonder has happened

01:03:17.280 --> 01:03:20.370
and that there's a broader
flattening of the information

01:03:20.370 --> 01:03:23.520
and a deeper
interaction that's now,

01:03:23.520 --> 01:03:27.900
I think, the real agenda
for me in the 21st century.

01:03:27.900 --> 01:03:30.180
The real agenda, I
say that as an issue

01:03:30.180 --> 01:03:31.890
because I think that
the densification

01:03:31.890 --> 01:03:33.810
of our urban
environments creates

01:03:33.810 --> 01:03:39.570
a new relationship where
this idea is now very, very

01:03:39.570 --> 01:03:40.380
important.

01:03:40.380 --> 01:03:43.797
The way in which cities change
has radically changed that we--

01:03:43.797 --> 01:03:46.380
The palace is no longer the only
building that's always there.

01:03:46.380 --> 01:03:51.480
The temporality of the effects
in the city have shifted.

01:03:51.480 --> 01:03:54.390
Are we now talking about
continually chasing that speed?

01:03:54.390 --> 01:03:56.070
Or are we talking
about different modes

01:03:56.070 --> 01:04:01.045
of working, which start to
engage in a different way?

01:04:01.045 --> 01:04:02.670
The practice, which
I think has already

01:04:02.670 --> 01:04:06.210
shifted in the makers of or the
producers of the content that

01:04:06.210 --> 01:04:08.081
are in those buildings.

01:04:10.850 --> 01:04:12.610
I also think in some
ways, when we're

01:04:12.610 --> 01:04:16.450
talking about the life and
the mission of the museum,

01:04:16.450 --> 01:04:20.590
this idea of wonder
and the familiar

01:04:20.590 --> 01:04:23.350
are somewhat a false dichotomy.

01:04:23.350 --> 01:04:26.020
Because it's not a question--

01:04:26.020 --> 01:04:30.100
The wonder is what exists within
the art and the art experience.

01:04:30.100 --> 01:04:32.650
That can still be
there and still

01:04:32.650 --> 01:04:36.350
the institution,
however, through access,

01:04:36.350 --> 01:04:39.243
can offer that opportunity.

01:04:39.243 --> 01:04:40.910
Really, it's not so
much that some of us

01:04:40.910 --> 01:04:43.560
are trying to make our
institutions familiar.

01:04:43.560 --> 01:04:46.010
We are trying to
make them accessible.

01:04:46.010 --> 01:04:48.830
That accessibility
is about how people

01:04:48.830 --> 01:04:50.990
understand the institution.

01:04:50.990 --> 01:04:52.910
That's all kinds of people.

01:04:52.910 --> 01:04:54.380
How they understand
the institution

01:04:54.380 --> 01:04:56.780
and what they understand
the experience to be.

01:04:56.780 --> 01:04:59.690
That really gets that some
of those much more practical

01:04:59.690 --> 01:05:00.690
issues as well.

01:05:00.690 --> 01:05:02.773
Now certainly, it's something
we've thought about,

01:05:02.773 --> 01:05:05.240
the architecture itself.

01:05:05.240 --> 01:05:06.800
How does the building speak?

01:05:06.800 --> 01:05:10.040
What is the experience of this
building versus the other kinds

01:05:10.040 --> 01:05:12.830
of experiences that people
have in architecture,

01:05:12.830 --> 01:05:14.650
say, in my local community?

01:05:14.650 --> 01:05:17.660
That's why bringing in
those ideas of those spaces

01:05:17.660 --> 01:05:18.630
was important.

01:05:18.630 --> 01:05:22.100
But I do think that when we're
thinking about a 21st century

01:05:22.100 --> 01:05:25.580
museum, we have to
reject some notions

01:05:25.580 --> 01:05:29.690
of this traditional idea of what
a museum was in order for us

01:05:29.690 --> 01:05:32.390
to really open out
to what a museum is.

01:05:32.390 --> 01:05:33.770
I think that's what--

01:05:33.770 --> 01:05:36.332
Charles referred to these
statistics the Broad has.

01:05:36.332 --> 01:05:37.790
They've been shared
with many of us

01:05:37.790 --> 01:05:40.830
by their director, Joanne
Heyler, and they're incredible.

01:05:40.830 --> 01:05:42.590
It's incredible
what's going on there

01:05:42.590 --> 01:05:45.070
in terms of, not just
these demographics he

01:05:45.070 --> 01:05:46.610
spoke but the age.

01:05:46.610 --> 01:05:49.640
The median age there is
30 years younger, at least

01:05:49.640 --> 01:05:51.570
than most other museums.

01:05:51.570 --> 01:05:53.780
The amount of time
people are deciding

01:05:53.780 --> 01:05:56.510
to go in advance, that
goes against this idea

01:05:56.510 --> 01:05:58.350
that millennials don't
make any decisions.

01:05:58.350 --> 01:05:59.990
[LAUGHTER]

01:05:59.990 --> 01:06:02.750
Like the moment they're
going, all of that.

01:06:02.750 --> 01:06:05.780
Even the way in which
one enters the building

01:06:05.780 --> 01:06:08.810
and begins to engage
with the ticket takers

01:06:08.810 --> 01:06:09.890
and into the galleries.

01:06:09.890 --> 01:06:13.070
All are about a different
way of creating.

01:06:13.070 --> 01:06:15.050
Still, the art being
the space of wonder.

01:06:15.050 --> 01:06:18.310
But how we enter the institution
and live in the institution

01:06:18.310 --> 01:06:19.567
is super different.

01:06:22.090 --> 01:06:25.580
Thelma, you and I were
at a conference in La

01:06:25.580 --> 01:06:29.210
where we heard a
lecture and we heard

01:06:29.210 --> 01:06:31.940
about the great artist,
Anna Deavere Smith,

01:06:31.940 --> 01:06:34.130
using the term
"radical welcome,"

01:06:34.130 --> 01:06:36.020
which comes from
the church movement,

01:06:36.020 --> 01:06:38.900
to talk about what
cultural organizations need

01:06:38.900 --> 01:06:41.990
to do to change this approach.

01:06:41.990 --> 01:06:45.020
And what does it mean to
embody that architecturally,

01:06:45.020 --> 01:06:47.630
programmatically,
experientially?

01:06:47.630 --> 01:06:50.450
I mean, I know that's
what we're trying

01:06:50.450 --> 01:06:52.610
to embody and to embrace.

01:06:52.610 --> 01:06:55.970
It requires change.

01:06:55.970 --> 01:06:57.650
It isn't automatic.

01:06:57.650 --> 01:06:58.220
It's work.

01:07:01.820 --> 01:07:04.440
The one thing that
I might challenge,

01:07:04.440 --> 01:07:07.010
though, is I don't
think most people are

01:07:07.010 --> 01:07:09.290
familiar with the
museum experience.

01:07:09.290 --> 01:07:10.340
I challenge that.

01:07:10.340 --> 01:07:13.580
I think most people are not
familiar with the museum

01:07:13.580 --> 01:07:14.780
experience.

01:07:14.780 --> 01:07:17.180
I think, actually,
a lot of our work

01:07:17.180 --> 01:07:21.440
is about breaking down
that across class and race

01:07:21.440 --> 01:07:24.740
and geography.

01:07:24.740 --> 01:07:28.040
Reminding ourselves
that we have--

01:07:28.040 --> 01:07:29.960
There's a really
big world out there

01:07:29.960 --> 01:07:32.090
that we're just
scratching the surface at.

01:07:32.090 --> 01:07:38.000
We've got lots and lots of
work to do in the future.

01:07:38.000 --> 01:07:40.130
I'll just add that
little something.

01:07:40.130 --> 01:07:43.580
I think every museum
experience has

01:07:43.580 --> 01:07:46.340
to be filled with
wonder even if it is

01:07:46.340 --> 01:07:48.830
something that feels familiar.

01:07:48.830 --> 01:07:51.920
I think that there's
always a balance.

01:07:51.920 --> 01:07:54.320
I agree with Thelma,
there's always wonder.

01:07:54.320 --> 01:07:58.400
I mean, I look at the Picasso
show that was put on at MoMA

01:07:58.400 --> 01:08:01.100
and you knew most
of those pieces.

01:08:01.100 --> 01:08:04.460
They were comforting in
that you recognized them.

01:08:04.460 --> 01:08:07.250
You could walk into a
room and say, oh wow.

01:08:07.250 --> 01:08:10.860
Then simply the way that they
were paired with other pieces

01:08:10.860 --> 01:08:12.950
and the way they were shown--

01:08:12.950 --> 01:08:14.540
Wonder.

01:08:14.540 --> 01:08:15.050
Beauty.

01:08:15.050 --> 01:08:15.980
Wonder.

01:08:15.980 --> 01:08:20.359
I think the institutions, and I
think Jill was saying this too.

01:08:20.359 --> 01:08:23.330
The institution is what
needs to feel familiar.

01:08:23.330 --> 01:08:28.590
The thing that you feel like
it's your family, you love it.

01:08:28.590 --> 01:08:30.240
I'll just leave by
saying, and we've

01:08:30.240 --> 01:08:34.760
got to get to technology today
and it goes to the millennials.

01:08:34.760 --> 01:08:37.640
How I think we all
are concerned perhaps

01:08:37.640 --> 01:08:42.960
that attention spans
are very little

01:08:42.960 --> 01:08:50.720
and that we're so
image-driven and media centric

01:08:50.720 --> 01:08:55.010
that we live on spectacle.

01:08:55.010 --> 01:09:00.319
We have to have the
wonder or whatever.

01:09:00.319 --> 01:09:03.319
I think that the
culture is changing.

01:09:03.319 --> 01:09:06.500
I think it's imperative that
we follow suit in the way

01:09:06.500 --> 01:09:08.569
that we think of museums.

01:09:08.569 --> 01:09:11.300
Or we offer something else.

01:09:11.300 --> 01:09:14.420
I think that that
could be a model.

01:09:14.420 --> 01:09:17.330
I'd like to add, I
think, with respect

01:09:17.330 --> 01:09:19.430
to that in terms of
technology and young people

01:09:19.430 --> 01:09:21.830
and having a teenager
and being aware

01:09:21.830 --> 01:09:25.069
of all these
collectives online that

01:09:25.069 --> 01:09:28.069
are run by teenagers
and people like maybe--

01:09:28.069 --> 01:09:29.479
19 is really old.

01:09:32.270 --> 01:09:34.950
Those are the older
founders and members.

01:09:34.950 --> 01:09:39.550
But that their engagement
of museums are all online.

01:09:39.550 --> 01:09:43.899
Their mission is really to
create this space online

01:09:43.899 --> 01:09:47.410
where they share information
that they get from archives

01:09:47.410 --> 01:09:50.205
from online sites, from
other institutions,

01:09:50.205 --> 01:09:52.330
and they can build their
own narratives about them.

01:09:52.330 --> 01:09:53.950
They can invite
other young artists

01:09:53.950 --> 01:09:55.618
that might be more isolated.

01:09:55.618 --> 01:09:57.910
They're on their computer
but they don't go to museums.

01:09:57.910 --> 01:10:01.520
They don't have this access
to the outside world.

01:10:01.520 --> 01:10:05.080
But they build their own
narratives and their own art

01:10:05.080 --> 01:10:08.890
historical canon about things
that they read and online

01:10:08.890 --> 01:10:11.560
about all these
different institutions.

01:10:11.560 --> 01:10:18.010
In some ways, I think the future
of this curatorial discourse

01:10:18.010 --> 01:10:23.020
within the museum has to engage
young people and their ideas

01:10:23.020 --> 01:10:27.070
about the archive and what
it represents to them now.

01:10:27.070 --> 01:10:31.090
That becomes a conversation
between the institution and--

01:10:31.090 --> 01:10:34.390
What happens in one
of these collectives

01:10:34.390 --> 01:10:35.890
that I'm thinking about.

01:10:35.890 --> 01:10:38.650
Between two founders,
one is 15 years old,

01:10:38.650 --> 01:10:41.793
the other is 17 years old.

01:10:41.793 --> 01:10:43.960
They were asked, when was
the first time you've met?

01:10:43.960 --> 01:10:45.002
They go, we've never met.

01:10:45.002 --> 01:10:47.165
We'll meet next Wednesday.

01:10:47.165 --> 01:10:49.540
Had never met in person but
they've been working together

01:10:49.540 --> 01:10:52.600
for two or three years.

01:10:52.600 --> 01:10:55.390
That is part of the reality.

01:10:55.390 --> 01:10:58.240
While place and community
is also important,

01:10:58.240 --> 01:11:02.290
engagement of this world
is also really important.

01:11:02.290 --> 01:11:04.810
Because they're
important voices that

01:11:04.810 --> 01:11:07.360
are broadening the
scope to other teenagers

01:11:07.360 --> 01:11:09.340
that don't have access
to young people, who are

01:11:09.340 --> 01:11:12.040
leading very isolated lives.

01:11:12.040 --> 01:11:13.430
It's an amazing thing.

01:11:13.430 --> 01:11:15.820
But do you see that as both/and?

01:11:15.820 --> 01:11:16.840
Not either/or.

01:11:16.840 --> 01:11:17.450
No, both/and.

01:11:17.450 --> 01:11:17.950
Yeah.

01:11:17.950 --> 01:11:18.640
Absolutely.

01:11:18.640 --> 01:11:19.390
Absolutely.

01:11:19.390 --> 01:11:21.940
With respect to those
developments then,

01:11:21.940 --> 01:11:25.900
do you see the physical
institutionalization

01:11:25.900 --> 01:11:29.470
of the museum, the
Studio Museum, the ICA,

01:11:29.470 --> 01:11:32.680
as is also constituting a risk?

01:11:32.680 --> 01:11:36.700
A limitation in the
sense that your--

01:11:40.210 --> 01:11:43.030
We've opened discussion
around flexibility.

01:11:43.030 --> 01:11:46.160
But flexibility in terms of
curatorial space and exhibition

01:11:46.160 --> 01:11:46.660
space.

01:11:46.660 --> 01:11:49.390
But there's another
conversation around flexibility

01:11:49.390 --> 01:11:53.290
in terms of making
concrete the institution.

01:11:53.290 --> 01:11:59.800
Is there any sense of, not lost
necessarily, but the finally

01:11:59.800 --> 01:12:02.980
realizing the dream of
the founders of both

01:12:02.980 --> 01:12:07.210
the ICA and the Studio Museum
for their museum of the future,

01:12:07.210 --> 01:12:11.290
represents a concreteness
that produces

01:12:11.290 --> 01:12:14.410
a certain static quality and
institution that has to be

01:12:14.410 --> 01:12:16.390
compensated in other ways?

01:12:16.390 --> 01:12:19.030
Clearly, you describe the way
in which the ICA has opened up

01:12:19.030 --> 01:12:22.510
all sorts of new possibilities
not only for exhibiting

01:12:22.510 --> 01:12:23.350
but for engaging--

01:12:23.350 --> 01:12:27.580
Community engaging, the city
engaging space and so forth.

01:12:27.580 --> 01:12:30.760
Do they immediately present
institutional limitations

01:12:30.760 --> 01:12:34.270
as you confront an audience
that is going to encounter

01:12:34.270 --> 01:12:35.230
the museum digitally?

01:12:35.230 --> 01:12:40.870
Encounter the museum in these
very unanticipated ways?

01:12:40.870 --> 01:12:43.540
I don't think that's
an issue as an artist.

01:12:43.540 --> 01:12:45.640
I think that's part
of the fabric that

01:12:45.640 --> 01:12:50.230
needs to be interwoven now as
part of what the museum does.

01:12:50.230 --> 01:12:51.550
But also part of there--

01:12:51.550 --> 01:12:55.048
It's their engagement.

01:12:55.048 --> 01:12:56.590
It's an archive that
you have online.

01:12:56.590 --> 01:12:58.923
You can go through the list
of artists and look at work.

01:12:58.923 --> 01:13:00.670
But somehow, the
institution also

01:13:00.670 --> 01:13:04.840
has to be responsive to that
community in some kind of way

01:13:04.840 --> 01:13:08.410
in conversation.

01:13:08.410 --> 01:13:11.200
Definitely, in terms
of many communities

01:13:11.200 --> 01:13:14.710
and as I was saying, these
isolated pockets that

01:13:14.710 --> 01:13:17.170
have no kind of
cultural structures

01:13:17.170 --> 01:13:19.780
in different communities,
it's really important

01:13:19.780 --> 01:13:20.560
to have a place.

01:13:20.560 --> 01:13:22.393
I don't think we can
get to a point and say,

01:13:22.393 --> 01:13:23.230
it doesn't matter.

01:13:23.230 --> 01:13:24.280
All you need is
something online.

01:13:24.280 --> 01:13:26.530
You don't really need an
institution that's in a place

01:13:26.530 --> 01:13:27.790
that people can go to.

01:13:27.790 --> 01:13:29.260
I think you have to have both.

01:13:29.260 --> 01:13:31.420
I think both are
hugely important.

01:13:31.420 --> 01:13:33.317
But it's about the
fabric in which

01:13:33.317 --> 01:13:34.900
you think of the
mission of the museum

01:13:34.900 --> 01:13:40.780
and the landscape of beyond
its boundary of its footprint.

01:13:40.780 --> 01:13:42.640
That digitally and
the internet is

01:13:42.640 --> 01:13:45.520
going to be a big part of
being responsive to a larger

01:13:45.520 --> 01:13:49.210
world that doesn't have access
even in their own communities,

01:13:49.210 --> 01:13:53.140
if they are even
in another state.

01:13:53.140 --> 01:13:56.440
I mean, I think also there's a--

01:13:56.440 --> 01:14:00.040
You can argue that almost every
building is, by default, out

01:14:00.040 --> 01:14:01.630
of date by the time it's made.

01:14:01.630 --> 01:14:05.414
Because in the end,
it's in the past.

01:14:05.414 --> 01:14:05.914
Don't worry.

01:14:05.914 --> 01:14:06.810
[LAUGHTER]

01:14:06.810 --> 01:14:07.540
No, no.

01:14:07.540 --> 01:14:09.340
I say that in a
very romantic way.

01:14:09.340 --> 01:14:15.610
[LAUGHTER]

01:14:15.610 --> 01:14:18.610
But I think that there
is something also

01:14:18.610 --> 01:14:21.260
extremely valuable about the
concretization and the made

01:14:21.260 --> 01:14:21.760
space.

01:14:21.760 --> 01:14:25.030
Because I think that
that is actually also--

01:14:25.030 --> 01:14:29.530
we are at the beginning
of a digital journey

01:14:29.530 --> 01:14:31.570
of understanding what
that means in terms

01:14:31.570 --> 01:14:33.340
of our spatial relationships.

01:14:33.340 --> 01:14:37.060
But architecture has always been
the device we actually trained

01:14:37.060 --> 01:14:43.090
civic narratives about how to
perform within those things.

01:14:43.090 --> 01:14:47.885
The concretization creates the
ritual, which creates, for me,

01:14:47.885 --> 01:14:49.510
the practice, which
then gets handed on

01:14:49.510 --> 01:14:53.050
to generations about
certain ways of performing

01:14:53.050 --> 01:14:54.195
within a civic.

01:14:54.195 --> 01:14:56.320
Of course, there are
disruptions that are happening

01:14:56.320 --> 01:14:57.640
when new technologies come.

01:14:57.640 --> 01:15:00.040
But the new technologies
also create their own rituals

01:15:00.040 --> 01:15:02.050
eventually.

01:15:02.050 --> 01:15:04.300
The beginning seem completely
vacuous to a generation

01:15:04.300 --> 01:15:07.030
who don't understand it
and very cognitive to one.

01:15:07.030 --> 01:15:09.520
But very quickly,
the gaps close.

01:15:09.520 --> 01:15:13.030
The gaps do close and then
there's another expansion.

01:15:13.030 --> 01:15:15.910
That's actually the
pleasure of the city.

01:15:15.910 --> 01:15:20.110
I think that that's actually--
these concretizing moments

01:15:20.110 --> 01:15:24.010
are really important to create
the rituals and patterns

01:15:24.010 --> 01:15:29.830
and to challenge past models but
also to physicalize new models.

01:15:29.830 --> 01:15:30.860
I couldn't agree more.

01:15:30.860 --> 01:15:34.570
I think your talk, how
you and Thelma are talking

01:15:34.570 --> 01:15:36.100
about the street
and the stoop, I

01:15:36.100 --> 01:15:41.770
think the museum parallels that
in the sense of this notion

01:15:41.770 --> 01:15:43.390
of human friction.

01:15:43.390 --> 01:15:46.090
That by having a certain
amount of density

01:15:46.090 --> 01:15:51.130
and colliding people who
may be unfamiliar, come

01:15:51.130 --> 01:15:54.730
from different places or
unexpected encounters,

01:15:54.730 --> 01:15:59.200
that from that friction
explodes ideas we don't yet know

01:15:59.200 --> 01:16:03.490
and people we don't
yet know and invention.

01:16:03.490 --> 01:16:05.560
I don't like the
innovation word anymore.

01:16:05.560 --> 01:16:06.640
[LAUGHS]

01:16:07.540 --> 01:16:10.000
But the possibilities,
that's why

01:16:10.000 --> 01:16:13.870
big cities have great artists
are attracted to them,

01:16:13.870 --> 01:16:15.040
thinkers.

01:16:15.040 --> 01:16:16.540
That human friction.

01:16:16.540 --> 01:16:19.720
I think the museum
is the site for that,

01:16:19.720 --> 01:16:21.280
similar to the street.

01:16:21.280 --> 01:16:24.010
But by having
walls around it, it

01:16:24.010 --> 01:16:28.930
has also a sense of that
you can take some risk

01:16:28.930 --> 01:16:30.730
within the protection of walls.

01:16:30.730 --> 01:16:35.950
I think that's an
important notion of place.

01:16:35.950 --> 01:16:37.990
It is that, but it's also--

01:16:37.990 --> 01:16:41.740
For us, in some ways, it's
about an institutional life.

01:16:41.740 --> 01:16:44.560
While I do believe
some of our colleagues

01:16:44.560 --> 01:16:46.480
are going to come to
an idea that there

01:16:46.480 --> 01:16:50.440
is a contemporary art museum
model that does not necessarily

01:16:50.440 --> 01:16:53.815
need physical spaces in the
way that Jill has one now

01:16:53.815 --> 01:16:56.560
or I am going to have one.

01:16:56.560 --> 01:16:58.960
But some of that
really has to do

01:16:58.960 --> 01:17:01.600
with a point in
institutional life.

01:17:01.600 --> 01:17:07.120
For us, just ahead of our
50th anniversary, architecture

01:17:07.120 --> 01:17:10.930
also became an important
way to mark, symbolically,

01:17:10.930 --> 01:17:13.810
the museum's permanence.

01:17:13.810 --> 01:17:16.940
We would not be able to do
this project, for example,

01:17:16.940 --> 01:17:19.780
if we did not own our building.

01:17:19.780 --> 01:17:23.260
The idea of buying a
building in Harlem in 1979

01:17:23.260 --> 01:17:25.120
would not have seemed,
to many people,

01:17:25.120 --> 01:17:28.540
like anything that made sense
from any number of reasons.

01:17:28.540 --> 01:17:31.690
But it was the museum's desire
to be not just a part of,

01:17:31.690 --> 01:17:34.240
but a real catalyst
to what was going

01:17:34.240 --> 01:17:37.825
to be seen as the possibility
of great economic development

01:17:37.825 --> 01:17:40.450
and a new kind of urbanism that
could come to the neighborhood.

01:17:40.450 --> 01:17:43.120
That owning the building
made that possible.

01:17:43.120 --> 01:17:44.510
Now the ownership
of the building

01:17:44.510 --> 01:17:46.900
is what makes it possible for
us to build a new building.

01:17:46.900 --> 01:17:48.400
We're actually
building on our site.

01:17:48.400 --> 01:17:51.280
Our building is coming down
and we're building directly

01:17:51.280 --> 01:17:53.950
on the site that we have been.

01:17:53.950 --> 01:17:57.460
It's also about this
idea of, it's not so much

01:17:57.460 --> 01:18:01.240
that we can say that
concretizing the space

01:18:01.240 --> 01:18:02.200
narrows us.

01:18:02.200 --> 01:18:04.060
It actually expands us.

01:18:04.060 --> 01:18:05.830
Because it's what
allows the permanence

01:18:05.830 --> 01:18:07.510
that says that we
can think about how

01:18:07.510 --> 01:18:10.400
to work in all these
very different ways.

01:18:10.400 --> 01:18:12.160
I think that speaks
in a way just directly

01:18:12.160 --> 01:18:15.280
to the title of the panel,
"Museum of the Future."

01:18:15.280 --> 01:18:18.160
It's not so much what
is the specific nature

01:18:18.160 --> 01:18:20.680
of that future space
but in 1979, there's

01:18:20.680 --> 01:18:23.335
a condition for the museum
of the future, which

01:18:23.335 --> 01:18:25.210
is the purchase of the
land and the provision

01:18:25.210 --> 01:18:28.330
of this possibility
at some future time.

01:18:28.330 --> 01:18:30.700
Then what are
thoughts on the museum

01:18:30.700 --> 01:18:32.260
of the future in those terms?

01:18:32.260 --> 01:18:34.490
Not the predictive,
what will it look like?

01:18:34.490 --> 01:18:36.520
But what are the
conditions of possibility

01:18:36.520 --> 01:18:39.850
that are really required
for institutions

01:18:39.850 --> 01:18:40.810
to think about now?

01:18:40.810 --> 01:18:42.360
Is there a question of scale?

01:18:42.360 --> 01:18:45.610
It's notable, I think, that all
of the examples we're seeing

01:18:45.610 --> 01:18:48.610
are institutions that have
very particular skies, a very

01:18:48.610 --> 01:18:53.110
particular scale, that
actually, in a way, maps

01:18:53.110 --> 01:18:54.760
onto the scale of a community.

01:18:54.760 --> 01:18:56.920
As opposed to the
Metropolitan or something.

01:18:56.920 --> 01:19:00.160
These much larger institutions
that have a different

01:19:00.160 --> 01:19:05.170
unwieldiness in relationship to
their putative constituencies.

01:19:05.170 --> 01:19:06.850
Not just scale but
other thoughts.

01:19:06.850 --> 01:19:09.312
What are the conditions
of possibility

01:19:09.312 --> 01:19:11.645
that need to be considered
for the museum of the future?

01:19:23.120 --> 01:19:31.070
I think museums have to
be local, by definition.

01:19:31.070 --> 01:19:32.720
I think that that's
one of the things

01:19:32.720 --> 01:19:35.960
that they get charged by.

01:19:35.960 --> 01:19:37.462
Is the way they
relate to the site,

01:19:37.462 --> 01:19:39.170
it's what you're
talking about the stoop,

01:19:39.170 --> 01:19:45.530
and how we use the ICA
and we're using Brazil.

01:19:45.530 --> 01:19:51.500
But somehow, they also
have to be universal.

01:19:51.500 --> 01:19:55.860
Bridging those two
things is tricky.

01:19:55.860 --> 01:20:00.041
It's a little difficult.

01:20:00.041 --> 01:20:05.570
The Shed, it's trying
to locate itself

01:20:05.570 --> 01:20:07.820
in the industrial
past of New York.

01:20:07.820 --> 01:20:10.730
It's on rail tracks that
parallel the High Line's rail

01:20:10.730 --> 01:20:11.750
tracks.

01:20:11.750 --> 01:20:16.040
It's using port
technology that used to,

01:20:16.040 --> 01:20:20.510
more or less, be on the Hudson.

01:20:20.510 --> 01:20:24.770
it's harking back
to a past in a part

01:20:24.770 --> 01:20:29.630
of the town that has obliterated
its entire past in a way.

01:20:29.630 --> 01:20:32.900
The Hudson Yards-- not in
Chelsea, but the Hudson Yards.

01:20:32.900 --> 01:20:34.790
But at the same
time, it's really

01:20:34.790 --> 01:20:39.410
trying to make a
universal space.

01:20:39.410 --> 01:20:46.190
As art that is traveling around
more and more and artists

01:20:46.190 --> 01:20:52.640
and curators are mobile
and international,

01:20:52.640 --> 01:20:57.710
the neutrality that
that may demand--

01:20:57.710 --> 01:21:00.410
I mean, I'm just going to
have this be a question.

01:21:00.410 --> 01:21:02.570
Is it a good thing
or a bad thing?

01:21:02.570 --> 01:21:05.600
Is column-free space a
good thing or a bad thing?

01:21:05.600 --> 01:21:08.390
Having systems that are
the same in Manchester

01:21:08.390 --> 01:21:11.030
as in Park Avenue Armory,
as in Culture Shed.

01:21:11.030 --> 01:21:14.180
Does that make
these places great

01:21:14.180 --> 01:21:17.720
because they can do everything
that's been done everywhere?

01:21:17.720 --> 01:21:23.720
Or does it make
them uninteresting?

01:21:23.720 --> 01:21:27.680
This would be a good
time maybe to refer

01:21:27.680 --> 01:21:29.450
to the slide we had up there.

01:21:29.450 --> 01:21:33.710
Because we're talking about
local and global museums.

01:21:33.710 --> 01:21:38.090
This is what happened at the
Guggenheim Museum in New York

01:21:38.090 --> 01:21:39.230
yesterday.

01:21:39.230 --> 01:21:40.040
I didn't see it.

01:21:40.040 --> 01:21:44.570
I received this by an email
by one of the people involved

01:21:44.570 --> 01:21:47.930
in Gulf Labor, which has
been in a big dispute

01:21:47.930 --> 01:21:53.660
with the Guggenheim over
labor practices in the design

01:21:53.660 --> 01:21:58.640
and construction for their
new museum in the Gulf.

01:21:58.640 --> 01:22:02.360
As well as NYU and
other institutions.

01:22:02.360 --> 01:22:05.210
It does speak to
the incredible, I

01:22:05.210 --> 01:22:07.670
think it's a complicated
story and I don't claim

01:22:07.670 --> 01:22:11.960
to be deep enough into it,
but the idea of working

01:22:11.960 --> 01:22:13.940
and where you are not--

01:22:13.940 --> 01:22:18.140
Where you're out of your element
in a really significant way.

01:22:18.140 --> 01:22:20.000
Having to deal
with labor, having

01:22:20.000 --> 01:22:23.600
to deal with issues of
immigration and pay,

01:22:23.600 --> 01:22:30.980
and to try to do this in
service of a universal museum

01:22:30.980 --> 01:22:33.320
with a global brand.

01:22:33.320 --> 01:22:36.110
You see that there is--

01:22:36.110 --> 01:22:41.270
This is another way to think
about the museum of the future

01:22:41.270 --> 01:22:44.300
in terms of the conflict
and the complexity

01:22:44.300 --> 01:22:47.093
that I think it presents.

01:22:47.093 --> 01:22:48.010
I couldn't agree more.

01:22:48.010 --> 01:22:53.280
I think that the discourse
ironically about the universal,

01:22:53.280 --> 01:22:56.180
I think actually is being
very highly questioned

01:22:56.180 --> 01:22:57.830
by a new crop of museums.

01:22:57.830 --> 01:23:02.505
Because if we are wanting a
world where topologies reduce

01:23:02.505 --> 01:23:05.330
and become so reductive
that they're just repeated

01:23:05.330 --> 01:23:08.750
around the world to tick
boxes, then the experience

01:23:08.750 --> 01:23:11.425
of the globe
becomes really flat.

01:23:11.425 --> 01:23:12.800
I actually think
that it's really

01:23:12.800 --> 01:23:15.140
ironic that in a
funny sort of way,

01:23:15.140 --> 01:23:18.050
this concentration on
the notion of place,

01:23:18.050 --> 01:23:21.770
and it could be banal,
things that seem banal,

01:23:21.770 --> 01:23:24.290
in the cold analysis.

01:23:24.290 --> 01:23:26.660
But actually, deeply
charge and make

01:23:26.660 --> 01:23:29.300
rich the experience of
that local condition

01:23:29.300 --> 01:23:31.850
are now becoming the
sought after tropes

01:23:31.850 --> 01:23:34.970
that actually people in
the planet are seeking.

01:23:34.970 --> 01:23:38.750
I don't seek to go see a big
palace anymore when I travel.

01:23:38.750 --> 01:23:41.510
I seek to go experience
a local condition

01:23:41.510 --> 01:23:45.170
or a food or a
particularity of a place.

01:23:45.170 --> 01:23:48.920
In a way, I think that
the modernist project has

01:23:48.920 --> 01:23:51.440
switched its game a little bit.

01:23:51.440 --> 01:23:54.060
It's no longer about this thing.

01:23:54.060 --> 01:23:58.790
It's actually finding a pleasure
in a world that's unifying

01:23:58.790 --> 01:24:02.630
and trying to create the
familiar has become special.

01:24:06.715 --> 01:24:08.840
The pleasure and the
particular has become actually

01:24:08.840 --> 01:24:12.440
much more heightened than
its universal spectacle.

01:24:12.440 --> 01:24:15.090
You could argue that maybe
that's a pendulum swing.

01:24:15.090 --> 01:24:18.000
There is a caution in
thinking about that.

01:24:18.000 --> 01:24:21.773
But I think that what is
not to be underestimated

01:24:21.773 --> 01:24:23.940
is the speed of construction
and urbanization, which

01:24:23.940 --> 01:24:24.660
is happening.

01:24:27.510 --> 01:24:32.440
We laugh at places like
Dubai for just quickly buying

01:24:32.440 --> 01:24:33.960
the Louvre or something else.

01:24:33.960 --> 01:24:36.690
But in a way, they are
reflecting a certain condition

01:24:36.690 --> 01:24:39.900
that's been set up 100
years before them, which

01:24:39.900 --> 01:24:42.540
is that somehow these
palaces would codify them

01:24:42.540 --> 01:24:45.390
as metropolitan centers.

01:24:45.390 --> 01:24:48.810
Now, we're having this moment
where this repetition of this.

01:24:48.810 --> 01:24:52.260
Replicating is having
the effect is getting

01:24:52.260 --> 01:24:54.240
a bit exhausting for us.

01:24:54.240 --> 01:24:58.050
Where there's a fatigue with
this idea of this thing.

01:24:58.050 --> 01:25:00.758
The irony is that this is
maybe a high conversation

01:25:00.758 --> 01:25:02.550
but I think that actually
this conversation

01:25:02.550 --> 01:25:03.842
will percolate down eventually.

01:25:03.842 --> 01:25:05.730
Because there is
this moment where

01:25:05.730 --> 01:25:08.250
there is a group, a large group
of people, who are thinking,

01:25:08.250 --> 01:25:10.260
my God, that thing
that was in New York

01:25:10.260 --> 01:25:13.110
only is suddenly now
in my neighborhood.

01:25:13.110 --> 01:25:15.660
It looks roughly familiar.

01:25:15.660 --> 01:25:19.980
This notion of the effects of
simulations in different places

01:25:19.980 --> 01:25:23.130
does have a power of attraction.

01:25:23.130 --> 01:25:24.607
Just a spectacle.

01:25:24.607 --> 01:25:26.190
I think that we
shouldn't be surprised

01:25:26.190 --> 01:25:29.720
when a spectacle lands or
when something feels special

01:25:29.720 --> 01:25:30.720
that it attracts people.

01:25:30.720 --> 01:25:32.460
I don't think the museum's
job is necessarily just

01:25:32.460 --> 01:25:33.360
to attract people.

01:25:33.360 --> 01:25:35.670
I actually think it's to
disseminate information

01:25:35.670 --> 01:25:41.250
and to create a way in which
that knowledge is actually

01:25:41.250 --> 01:25:44.610
given to the most as a
way of making a society.

01:25:44.610 --> 01:25:46.290
I really think its
fundamental job

01:25:46.290 --> 01:25:50.010
is to create a certain
cultural body, which creates

01:25:50.010 --> 01:25:52.020
a certain society that we want.

01:25:52.020 --> 01:25:56.250
Rather than just a place
to engage a stimulation.

01:25:56.250 --> 01:25:59.490
That's a very different kind
of thing, I would argue.

01:25:59.490 --> 01:26:03.990
I think that's really, for
me, the conversation that's

01:26:03.990 --> 01:26:04.560
coming here.

01:26:04.560 --> 01:26:07.590
I think that the museum
is actually growing up

01:26:07.590 --> 01:26:10.200
from just trying to just
attract to becoming a much more

01:26:10.200 --> 01:26:11.940
precise tool.

01:26:11.940 --> 01:26:14.830
I find that fascinating.

01:26:14.830 --> 01:26:16.960
If I could ask one
last question before we

01:26:16.960 --> 01:26:18.880
turn it over to the audience.

01:26:18.880 --> 01:26:22.960
It deals with
institutional critique.

01:26:22.960 --> 01:26:25.780
We were talking about the
friction and the friction

01:26:25.780 --> 01:26:26.950
within the museum.

01:26:26.950 --> 01:26:30.580
I'm thinking back to
Fred Wilson's Mining

01:26:30.580 --> 01:26:34.540
the Museum project,
where at that time,

01:26:34.540 --> 01:26:38.650
the institution of the
museum opened itself up

01:26:38.650 --> 01:26:43.120
to institutional critique
within it through an artist who

01:26:43.120 --> 01:26:48.100
is displacing artifacts to
create shocking juxtapositions,

01:26:48.100 --> 01:26:49.480
et cetera.

01:26:49.480 --> 01:26:54.130
Now we've talked about the
museum becoming a catalyst,

01:26:54.130 --> 01:26:58.570
becoming familiar, becoming
commodified, et cetera.

01:26:58.570 --> 01:27:00.745
I guess the question
I have is, is there

01:27:00.745 --> 01:27:03.640
a room for
institutional critique

01:27:03.640 --> 01:27:06.910
now that we have
arrived 100 years after

01:27:06.910 --> 01:27:14.290
and museums have become both
established these underdog

01:27:14.290 --> 01:27:18.370
museums that had cultural
critique embodied

01:27:18.370 --> 01:27:20.620
in it as an institution?

01:27:20.620 --> 01:27:24.070
And now, what are
those opportunities

01:27:24.070 --> 01:27:25.555
for institutional critique?

01:27:28.680 --> 01:27:34.460
I mean, the idea that we
would be institutionalized--

01:27:34.460 --> 01:27:36.420
[LAUGHTER]

01:27:36.420 --> 01:27:38.490
It seems a far stretch
for me because I

01:27:38.490 --> 01:27:44.730
feel like we are pretty
vulnerable and risk-embracing.

01:27:44.730 --> 01:27:47.760
Maybe we would love a
little less risk sometimes,

01:27:47.760 --> 01:27:49.930
by definition.

01:27:49.930 --> 01:27:55.650
I don't experience it that
way, let me just say that.

01:27:55.650 --> 01:27:58.950
I think that happens
through artists.

01:27:58.950 --> 01:28:01.890
I think that we've got
plenty of great ones

01:28:01.890 --> 01:28:08.520
doing that and giving them
the opportunity, the space,

01:28:08.520 --> 01:28:12.300
I actually think we do that
through our young people who

01:28:12.300 --> 01:28:15.270
are in the museums who bring
a critique of their own.

01:28:15.270 --> 01:28:19.230
And through our
leadership, which

01:28:19.230 --> 01:28:23.070
is all very challenging work.

01:28:23.070 --> 01:28:24.450
I mean, you marked the time.

01:28:24.450 --> 01:28:27.300
'92 is when Fred's Mining
the Museum happened.

01:28:27.300 --> 01:28:29.430
A year later, the '93
Biennial happened.

01:28:29.430 --> 01:28:32.340
It changed curatorial practice
and made curatorial practice

01:28:32.340 --> 01:28:35.267
inherently
institutionally critical.

01:28:35.267 --> 01:28:37.350
I think some of what we're
talking about here when

01:28:37.350 --> 01:28:39.870
we talk about the future of
museums, it's happening--

01:28:39.870 --> 01:28:42.510
One, on this level
of the architectural.

01:28:42.510 --> 01:28:44.490
We're seeing this boom,
and I'm a part of it,

01:28:44.490 --> 01:28:46.560
of this building of new museums.

01:28:46.560 --> 01:28:49.590
The physical space is
changing, transforming.

01:28:49.590 --> 01:28:52.530
But at the same time, as
Jill and I have experienced,

01:28:52.530 --> 01:28:55.230
in our field, museum
directors and curators,

01:28:55.230 --> 01:28:57.870
are rethinking
what our roles are.

01:28:57.870 --> 01:29:01.290
The old model of preserve,
present, collect, interpret,

01:29:01.290 --> 01:29:05.100
has really changed to think
about engage, enlighten,

01:29:05.100 --> 01:29:06.750
inspire.

01:29:06.750 --> 01:29:09.780
There are ways in
which that also

01:29:09.780 --> 01:29:12.820
is an institutional critique.

01:29:12.820 --> 01:29:15.360
The way in which we
all embody mission

01:29:15.360 --> 01:29:18.210
differently in our
institutions because

01:29:18.210 --> 01:29:22.170
at core, curatorial
practice is demanding that.

01:29:22.170 --> 01:29:25.350
I would also say the
old model of museums

01:29:25.350 --> 01:29:27.180
doesn't look like Thelma and me.

01:29:27.180 --> 01:29:28.135
Right.

01:29:28.135 --> 01:29:30.510
I was going to say, I'm just
asking those hypothetically.

01:29:30.510 --> 01:29:31.170
We are not--

01:29:31.170 --> 01:29:34.410
But in fact, the part
of what the change is

01:29:34.410 --> 01:29:38.880
is who are the people
who are shaping

01:29:38.880 --> 01:29:40.350
the field going forward?

01:29:40.350 --> 01:29:44.370
Taking the risks and succeeding?

01:29:44.370 --> 01:29:47.640
Bringing the thought
leadership that Thelma does.

01:29:47.640 --> 01:29:49.812
Changing our field.

01:29:49.812 --> 01:29:51.270
We all do it in
our different ways.

01:29:51.270 --> 01:29:56.250
But, I mean, is absolutely
inimitable and incredibly

01:29:56.250 --> 01:29:58.320
urgent and important.

01:29:58.320 --> 01:30:03.900
I think we're not right
here at risk for that.

01:30:03.900 --> 01:30:08.370
Can I just add that
I also think that

01:30:08.370 --> 01:30:12.167
that institutional critique
that happens with artists,

01:30:12.167 --> 01:30:14.250
and artists are the ones
who challenge that thing,

01:30:14.250 --> 01:30:16.710
is a wonderful cycle
of the whole thing.

01:30:16.710 --> 01:30:18.780
Just when we think
we've solved it,

01:30:18.780 --> 01:30:22.200
artists come in
and slap up around.

01:30:22.200 --> 01:30:25.620
Actually, you haven't
and the thing has moved.

01:30:25.620 --> 01:30:28.740
I thank God that artists do
that because otherwise, we

01:30:28.740 --> 01:30:32.340
would create entropies that
would be deeply problematic.

01:30:32.340 --> 01:30:34.778
They're continually
being remade.

01:30:38.350 --> 01:30:40.320
What is, I think, a
very unique moment right

01:30:40.320 --> 01:30:44.160
now is that there is this birth
of these new types of museums.

01:30:44.160 --> 01:30:47.520
I think that that is a wonderful
moment at the beginning

01:30:47.520 --> 01:30:48.765
of the century.

01:30:48.765 --> 01:30:52.530
On one way, we're
really forming something

01:30:52.530 --> 01:30:55.890
which will be toppled in
maybe 50 or 60 years time,

01:30:55.890 --> 01:30:59.520
might be 20 depending on the
speed of how things develop.

01:30:59.520 --> 01:31:03.120
But it's amazing to
be witnessing that.

01:31:03.120 --> 01:31:04.740
I would say that
the people who are

01:31:04.740 --> 01:31:07.470
on our boards, who
are our partners,

01:31:07.470 --> 01:31:11.040
are equally willing
to embrace risk.

01:31:11.040 --> 01:31:14.040
That, too, is I think a
change that we're both

01:31:14.040 --> 01:31:15.810
seeing and creating.

01:31:15.810 --> 01:31:19.020
But it takes a lot of people.

01:31:19.020 --> 01:31:22.980
I know creative time
has its little tagline

01:31:22.980 --> 01:31:26.940
they were that says, visionary
art needs visionary funders.

01:31:26.940 --> 01:31:29.370
But I steal that all
the time because I

01:31:29.370 --> 01:31:32.940
think it's absolutely true.

01:31:32.940 --> 01:31:38.220
We're each creating communities
that are working together

01:31:38.220 --> 01:31:40.890
to make change in
our neighborhoods

01:31:40.890 --> 01:31:41.640
and in our places.

01:31:43.772 --> 01:31:46.230
We're going to take a couple
of questions from the audience

01:31:46.230 --> 01:31:48.270
before we conclude, if we can.

01:31:53.670 --> 01:31:58.890
Ticket price is a huge
barrier to accessibility.

01:31:58.890 --> 01:32:03.150
In what way can low
or cheap tickets

01:32:03.150 --> 01:32:06.720
be designed into the
museum of the future?

01:32:06.720 --> 01:32:11.100
Yeah, I think the
future for most of us

01:32:11.100 --> 01:32:13.950
is an example that, it's why
we're all looking at the Broad,

01:32:13.950 --> 01:32:15.240
is being free.

01:32:15.240 --> 01:32:18.900
But that takes a very
visionary funding structure.

01:32:18.900 --> 01:32:22.120
It takes an important
endowment structure.

01:32:22.120 --> 01:32:24.780
But I think, again, in the shift
in the way in which we think

01:32:24.780 --> 01:32:27.690
of ourselves, who
we think we are,

01:32:27.690 --> 01:32:29.430
who we think we're
serving, it becomes

01:32:29.430 --> 01:32:33.270
almost impossible not
to think of making

01:32:33.270 --> 01:32:37.110
it possible for the museum to
be free to really reach mission.

01:32:37.110 --> 01:32:42.240
I think it hasn't happened
as radically as some of us

01:32:42.240 --> 01:32:42.930
might hope.

01:32:42.930 --> 01:32:45.900
Most of us have been able to
make this happen with fully

01:32:45.900 --> 01:32:47.910
free days, for example.

01:32:47.910 --> 01:32:49.685
The idea there is to
start with one day

01:32:49.685 --> 01:32:52.380
and you'll get to
seven at some point.

01:32:52.380 --> 01:32:55.460
But it is a place at which
you are exactly right.

01:32:55.460 --> 01:32:59.100
That is one of the
number one ways

01:32:59.100 --> 01:33:01.770
in which we can
create accessibility.

01:33:01.770 --> 01:33:04.530
We know it works because
when we see it in action

01:33:04.530 --> 01:33:07.890
is when we see audiences
that are broad and diverse

01:33:07.890 --> 01:33:10.830
and are also engaged.

01:33:10.830 --> 01:33:12.720
Because of the sense
of welcome that

01:33:12.720 --> 01:33:15.100
comes because they have access.

01:33:15.100 --> 01:33:18.180
I'd just like to
add, museums have

01:33:18.180 --> 01:33:21.630
to operate with
balanced budgets.

01:33:21.630 --> 01:33:24.930
While admission is actually
very small percentage

01:33:24.930 --> 01:33:28.770
of what runs museums or
what gives them money,

01:33:28.770 --> 01:33:30.620
you have to replace
it with something.

01:33:30.620 --> 01:33:34.230
I think one of the nasty
facts about museums

01:33:34.230 --> 01:33:38.160
these days is the
buildings get rented out.

01:33:38.160 --> 01:33:40.110
There's strange
affiliations that

01:33:40.110 --> 01:33:43.590
happen between
corporations and museums.

01:33:43.590 --> 01:33:46.710
They may not all be perfect
but they're necessary.

01:33:46.710 --> 01:33:49.710
I think we actually
have to deal with that.

01:33:52.350 --> 01:33:53.910
Dealing with that
is something that

01:33:53.910 --> 01:33:58.650
would allow museums to be
more accessible to a larger

01:33:58.650 --> 01:34:01.590
percentage of the population.

01:34:01.590 --> 01:34:03.740
Trying to think of
a second question.

01:34:03.740 --> 01:34:05.046
Right here.

01:34:05.046 --> 01:34:08.510
Hi, I have a question about
the idea of knowledge.

01:34:08.510 --> 01:34:11.060
Going to a museum is always
entertaining and nice.

01:34:11.060 --> 01:34:14.670
But about 10 years ago,
when you go to a museum,

01:34:14.670 --> 01:34:17.480
you would have a certain
amount of knowledge,

01:34:17.480 --> 01:34:20.330
like learning how to see
the art pieces so you

01:34:20.330 --> 01:34:21.770
would have some
kind of knowledge

01:34:21.770 --> 01:34:23.570
before you go to the museum.

01:34:23.570 --> 01:34:25.670
But these days, when
you have, for example,

01:34:25.670 --> 01:34:27.620
30 minutes at the
Louvre, what you would do

01:34:27.620 --> 01:34:30.750
is go in, rush into the
Mona Lisa, take a selfie--

01:34:30.750 --> 01:34:32.450
a lot of Millennials would do--

01:34:32.450 --> 01:34:33.890
and go back out.

01:34:33.890 --> 01:34:36.910
And my question is, as
the generations change,

01:34:36.910 --> 01:34:39.170
the amount knowledge
they will have

01:34:39.170 --> 01:34:41.340
before entering the
museum will be different,

01:34:41.340 --> 01:34:44.300
so how the museum of
future should address that?

01:34:44.300 --> 01:34:49.020
Possibly with technology
or with any other means?

01:34:49.020 --> 01:34:50.962
No, you're right.

01:34:50.962 --> 01:34:52.920
Because it's a great
question and I don't think

01:34:52.920 --> 01:34:53.520
we have an answer.

01:34:53.520 --> 01:34:55.145
But I think you are
asking the question

01:34:55.145 --> 01:34:56.160
we're asking ourselves.

01:34:56.160 --> 01:34:57.827
There are a few points
in your question,

01:34:57.827 --> 01:35:00.120
however, that are
interesting and that we are

01:35:00.120 --> 01:35:02.070
seeing in a generational shift.

01:35:02.070 --> 01:35:04.170
As Lorna spoke
about, young people

01:35:04.170 --> 01:35:06.780
are spending more time
engaging with art online.

01:35:06.780 --> 01:35:12.840
They do come with a certain
image bank in their minds

01:35:12.840 --> 01:35:15.840
when they're entering
into institutions.

01:35:15.840 --> 01:35:19.710
Quite often, it's what brings
them into the institution.

01:35:19.710 --> 01:35:23.130
Many of us see, for example,
the trail of social media

01:35:23.130 --> 01:35:25.770
and the way in
which that will take

01:35:25.770 --> 01:35:29.070
what is the image on the digital
screen into the experience

01:35:29.070 --> 01:35:32.640
of being in a place with other
people with those works of art

01:35:32.640 --> 01:35:34.140
and being able to discuss them.

01:35:34.140 --> 01:35:35.850
What I don't think
we've figured out

01:35:35.850 --> 01:35:39.330
is how that becomes
a seamless process,

01:35:39.330 --> 01:35:41.970
between that digital experience
and the one in the museum.

01:35:41.970 --> 01:35:45.750
And how we might shift the
experience of the museum

01:35:45.750 --> 01:35:47.650
to really galvanize
in a better way

01:35:47.650 --> 01:35:50.580
the ability to allow
people all the information

01:35:50.580 --> 01:35:54.000
they want, so that they
can gain more knowledge

01:35:54.000 --> 01:35:56.130
as they're engaging
with those works of art.

01:35:56.130 --> 01:35:59.910
But we're all experimenting
and experimenting wildly

01:35:59.910 --> 01:36:02.430
with some of this in
the hope that we're

01:36:02.430 --> 01:36:05.100
getting somewhere in a change
in the way in which this

01:36:05.100 --> 01:36:07.436
all will work.

01:36:07.436 --> 01:36:10.120
Do we have time for one more?

01:36:10.120 --> 01:36:11.860
OK.

01:36:11.860 --> 01:36:16.600
I'm very fascinated in
listening to the different talks

01:36:16.600 --> 01:36:20.620
about how a number of people
talked about the museum

01:36:20.620 --> 01:36:23.170
as a space of performance.

01:36:23.170 --> 01:36:26.260
In terms of performance,
for me and theater,

01:36:26.260 --> 01:36:28.510
there are different
types of theaters.

01:36:28.510 --> 01:36:30.880
There's the theater
of the castle, which

01:36:30.880 --> 01:36:35.170
has the King's perspective or
the royal perspective, that

01:36:35.170 --> 01:36:38.500
allows for the ideal
view from only one

01:36:38.500 --> 01:36:41.410
place in the assemblage.

01:36:41.410 --> 01:36:44.680
Versus the view of
collectivity that we

01:36:44.680 --> 01:36:48.430
have in the Greek theater,
where the audience itself,

01:36:48.430 --> 01:36:52.720
the assemblage of people,
is actually the show.

01:36:52.720 --> 01:36:55.420
In listening to the talk
and the range of questions

01:36:55.420 --> 01:36:58.300
and so forth, I see us
going back and forth

01:36:58.300 --> 01:37:02.140
as a culture between the
castle and the collectivity.

01:37:02.140 --> 01:37:06.370
I'm wondering,
where, in the shift,

01:37:06.370 --> 01:37:10.450
in the 21st century shift or
perhaps in the 20th century

01:37:10.450 --> 01:37:14.590
shift, those two
came into alignment?

01:37:14.590 --> 01:37:17.770
The castle which
limits the collectivity

01:37:17.770 --> 01:37:21.620
versus the Greek
theater of democracy,

01:37:21.620 --> 01:37:24.850
which is always looking to
increase the collectivity.

01:37:28.119 --> 01:37:29.987
Should I go?

01:37:29.987 --> 01:37:31.810
This one seems--

01:37:31.810 --> 01:37:33.582
Bring us home.

01:37:33.582 --> 01:37:36.227
[LAUGHTER]

01:37:36.227 --> 01:37:38.810
I mean, I think I was trying to
talk about that when we talked

01:37:38.810 --> 01:37:40.102
about the notion of the palace.

01:37:40.102 --> 01:37:41.428
Yes.

01:37:41.428 --> 01:37:43.220
If you look at the
evolution of the museum,

01:37:43.220 --> 01:37:47.580
you could argue that
modernism attempted

01:37:47.580 --> 01:37:54.440
to blend and to upend the
notion of the panoptic view.

01:37:54.440 --> 01:37:56.120
That was the modernist project.

01:37:56.120 --> 01:37:58.640
Everything right through to--

01:37:58.640 --> 01:38:00.290
I argue that
actually the Pompidou

01:38:00.290 --> 01:38:02.780
is a very important point
more than the Guggenheim

01:38:02.780 --> 01:38:05.690
in understanding the
evolution of museums.

01:38:05.690 --> 01:38:09.410
Because the Pompidou creates
the largest single span,

01:38:09.410 --> 01:38:14.570
flattest, most accessible,
plate to democratize art

01:38:14.570 --> 01:38:17.000
that has actually been done.

01:38:17.000 --> 01:38:19.760
It uses technology as a way
to infrastructure and support

01:38:19.760 --> 01:38:21.290
that.

01:38:21.290 --> 01:38:24.230
Then at that moment,
there's a pause.

01:38:24.230 --> 01:38:26.018
I don't think much has
happened after that

01:38:26.018 --> 01:38:27.560
and we're trying to
learn after that.

01:38:27.560 --> 01:38:29.810
Because I think what happens
is that there's then now

01:38:29.810 --> 01:38:36.470
the question of fatigue and
scale and relentlessness.

01:38:36.470 --> 01:38:41.870
When does just flattening
it also have its limits?

01:38:41.870 --> 01:38:44.210
I think that that's
also now creating

01:38:44.210 --> 01:38:47.630
polarities where you have
the Guggenheim, which

01:38:47.630 --> 01:38:52.220
was the broach, the object
as a specific destination

01:38:52.220 --> 01:38:54.440
with its ornamental quality.

01:38:54.440 --> 01:38:58.700
Then there's another discussion
about maybe a much more--

01:38:58.700 --> 01:39:01.630
I think the rise of ICAs, which
is a contemporary condition,

01:39:01.630 --> 01:39:04.520
and non-collecting
institutions are

01:39:04.520 --> 01:39:08.840
a response to that relentless
large, overwhelming condition.

01:39:08.840 --> 01:39:12.350
To create much more boutique,
much more curated, much

01:39:12.350 --> 01:39:16.820
more experiential moments
which are targeted to empower.

01:39:16.820 --> 01:39:18.350
There is an effort
that's happening

01:39:18.350 --> 01:39:20.510
within architecture
and museology

01:39:20.510 --> 01:39:22.567
to deal with this
democratization.

01:39:22.567 --> 01:39:24.650
But I think that, actually
what you're hitting at,

01:39:24.650 --> 01:39:27.170
which I think is very
important, is that ultimately,

01:39:27.170 --> 01:39:29.660
art's mission is
to try and bring

01:39:29.660 --> 01:39:33.800
that other part of our
lives into the everyday.

01:39:33.800 --> 01:39:36.890
It's not to make it rarified
into the holy of holies

01:39:36.890 --> 01:39:39.620
of the chapel or in
the palace of the King.

01:39:39.620 --> 01:39:42.140
But to actually have that
as an everyday experience

01:39:42.140 --> 01:39:45.380
for everyone because it
actually creates a richer

01:39:45.380 --> 01:39:49.170
condition for our democracies.

01:39:49.170 --> 01:39:51.020
[APPLAUSE]

